{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JTg2hqMfRUtR8GkQspAZrA","type":"tool_result","content":"# The excess phase diagram of MOSP\n\n*Ralph loop0010, executing `reports/excess_phase_plan.md`. One section per\nitem; each states its size range, its regenerate command, its kill verdict\nand what it does not claim. The parameter throughout is the excess\n`e = (n_ones − m) / n`, the mean number of patterns per customer minus one,\nalways realised from the matrix and never the generator's nominal value.*\n\n----\n% Appendix E: the first-moment lower bound on the pathwidth of sparse random\n% graphs, in plain language (owner's request, 2026-10-31). Source:\n% reports/random_graph_first_moment.md (Theorems A, A', A'' and the literature\n% verdict); Lean: EmptyPair.lean, FirstMoment.lean, FirstMomentAsymptotics.lean.\n% The beta*(c) values: reports/scratch/first_moment/beta_star.log (two codes agree).\n\\section{The pathwidth of sparse random graphs}\n\\label{app:random}\n\nThe equivalences of Section~\\ref{sec:equiv} make every statement about the\npathwidth of a graph a statement about the open stacks of an instance. This\nappendix proves one such statement for random graphs, in plain language, and\nrecords what of it is checked in Lean. The result is that a sparse random\ngraph has pathwidth proportional to its number of vertices, with an explicit\nconstant. The argument is a first moment, an old tool; what is new here is the\nexact form of its deterministic step, the constant it yields, and the\nmachine check. We found no prior statement of the constant.\n\n\\subsection{The statement}\n\n$G(n,p)$ is the random graph on $n$ labelled vertices in which each of the\n$\\binom n2$ possible edges is present with probability $p$, independently. We\ntake $p=c/n$, so that a vertex has about $c$ neighbours. Write\n$\\mathrm{pw}$ for pathwidth, $\\ln$ for the natural logarithm, and\n\\[\n  \\phi(x) = -x\\ln x \\quad(\\phi(0)=0),\\qquad\n  H(x,y,z) = \\phi(x)+\\phi(y)+\\phi(z)\n\\]\nfor the entropy of a probability vector with three entries.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[first moment, finite form]\n\\label{thm:firstmoment}\nLet $c>0$ and $0<\\beta<1$, and put $a=(1-\\beta)/2$ and\n\\[\n  \\delta(\\beta,c) \\;=\\; c\\,a^{2} \\;-\\; H(a,a,1-2a).\n\\]\nFor every integer $n\\ge\\max(3,c)$,\n\\[\n  \\Pr\\bigl[\\mathrm{pw}(G(n,c/n))\\le\\beta n\\bigr]\n  \\;\\le\\; \\exp\\bigl(-\\delta(\\beta,c)\\,n+3\\ln n+c\\,a\\bigr)\n  \\;\\le\\; e^{c/2}\\,n^{3}\\,e^{-\\delta(\\beta,c)\\,n}.\n\\]\n\\end{theorem}\n\nSo whenever $\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$, the probability that the pathwidth is as small\nas $\\beta n$ is exponentially small, and the pathwidth is at least $\\beta n$\nwith high probability. The next statement says for which $\\beta$ that happens.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the admissible region]\n\\label{thm:betastar}\nIf $c\\le4\\ln2$ there is no $\\beta\\in(0,1)$ with $\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$. If\n$c>4\\ln2$ there is a number $\\beta^{*}(c)\\in(0,1)$ such that\n$\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$ exactly when $\\beta<\\beta^{*}(c)$; it is the root in\n$(0,1)$ of\n\\[\n  -(1-\\beta)\\ln\\tfrac{1-\\beta}{2}-\\beta\\ln\\beta \\;=\\; \\tfrac{c}{4}(1-\\beta)^{2}.\n\\]\nHence $\\mathrm{pw}(G(n,c/n))\\ge\\beta n$ with probability $1-O(n^{3}e^{-\\delta n})$\nfor every $\\beta<\\beta^{*}(c)$, and as $c\\to\\infty$,\n$1-\\beta^{*}(c)=\\bigl(4\\ln c-4\\ln\\ln c+O(1)\\bigr)/c$.\n\\end{theorem}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:betastar} gives $\\beta^{*}(c)$ at a few values of $c$, beside the\nonly published explicit lower bound we know for a width parameter of\n$G(n,c/n)$, the treewidth bound $1-\\sqrt{9\\ln3/(2c)}$ of\n\\citet{PerarnauSerra2014}, which is a lower bound on pathwidth too since\ntreewidth never exceeds pathwidth.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The constant $\\beta^{*}(c)$ of Theorem~\\ref{thm:betastar}, to four\ndecimals, and the treewidth bound of \\citet{PerarnauSerra2014} at the same\n$c$ (blank where that bound is not positive). Two independent programs agree\non every digit of $\\beta^{*}$.}\n\\label{tab:betastar}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrrr@{}}\n\\toprule\n$c$ & 3 & 4 & 6 & 8 & 10 & 20 & 50 & 100 \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\beta^{*}(c)$ & 0.0087 & 0.0613 & 0.1706 & 0.2628 & 0.3371 & 0.5535 & 0.7606 & 0.8574 \\\\\n$1-\\sqrt{9\\ln3/(2c)}$ & & & 0.0918 & 0.2136 & 0.2966 & 0.5027 & 0.6855 & 0.7776 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe threshold $4\\ln2\\approx2.77$ in Theorem~\\ref{thm:betastar} belongs to the\nmethod, not to the graphs: it is the point at which an edge-free split of the\nvertices into two equal halves stops being likely, and below it the first\nmoment simply says nothing. The pathwidth of $G(n,c/n)$ is in fact linear in\n$n$ for every $c>1$ \\citep{LeeLeeOum2012}, with a constant that no one has\nmade explicit.\n\n\\subsection{The proof, in four steps}\n\n\\paragraph{Step 1: a narrow layout leaves an empty pair.}\nPut the vertices in a line and look at any position $i$. The first $i+1$\nvertices are the prefix; the rest are the suffix. A suffix vertex is\n\\emph{active} if it has a neighbour in the prefix. The vertex separation of\nthe line is the largest number of active vertices at any position, and by\nTheorem~\\ref{thm:vs} the smallest vertex separation over all lines is the\npathwidth. Now take a line of vertex separation $k$ and any position $i$. The\nsuffix has $n-i-1$ vertices, at most $k$ of them active, so at least $n-i-1-k$\nsuffix vertices have no neighbour at all in the prefix. Call that set $R$ and\nthe prefix $A$. Then $A$ and $R$ are disjoint, $|A|=i+1$, $|R|\\ge n-i-1-k$, and\nthere is no edge between them: an \\emph{empty pair}. This holds at every\nposition of every line, so a graph of pathwidth $k$ has an empty pair with\n$|A|=t$ and $|R|\\ge n-t-k$ for every $t\\le n$. With $t=\\lceil(n-k)/2\\rceil$\nboth parts have about $(n-k)/2$ vertices~\\leanref{emptypair}.\n\n\\paragraph{Step 2: count the graphs that have one.}\nFix a pair of disjoint sets $A$ and $R$ with $|A|=t$ and $|R|=t-1$. In\n$G(n,p)$ the $t(t-1)$ possible edges between them are all absent with\nprobability $(1-p)^{t(t-1)}$. There are $\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}$ such\npairs. A graph of pathwidth at most $k$ has at least one of them empty, so by\nthe union bound\n\\[\n  \\Pr[\\mathrm{pw}\\le k]\\;\\le\\;\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}\\,(1-p)^{t(t-1)},\n  \\qquad t=\\lceil(n-k)/2\\rceil .\n\\]\nThis is the whole probabilistic content of the proof, and it is a Lean\ntheorem over the finite sum of the weights $p^{|E|}(1-p)^{\\binom n2-|E|}$ of\nall graphs on $n$ vertices, with no probability model needed; the count of\ngraphs with a given empty pair is exact there, $2^{\\binom n2-t(t-1)}$ of\nthem~\\leanref{firstmoment}.\n\n\\paragraph{Step 3: estimate the count.}\nTwo standard inequalities turn the right-hand side into an exponential in\n$n$. The number of ways to choose the pair is the trinomial coefficient\n$n!/\\bigl(t!\\,(t-1)!\\,(n-2t+1)!\\bigr)$, and a trinomial coefficient is at most\n$\\exp(nH)$ with $H$ the entropy of the three proportions $t/n$, $(t-1)/n$ and\n$(n-2t+1)/n$ \\citep[Theorem~11.1.3]{CoverThomas2006}: expand\n$(x+y+z)^{n}=1$ at $x=t/n$, $y=(t-1)/n$, $z=(n-2t+1)/n$ and keep one term of\nthe sum. And $(1-p)^{m}\\le e^{-pm}$ because $1-p\\le e^{-p}$. With $p=c/n$ the\nsecond factor is at most $\\exp(-c\\,t(t-1)/n)$.\n\n\\paragraph{Step 4: the bookkeeping.}\nThe three proportions are not exactly $a$, $a$ and $1-2a$: with\n$k=\\lfloor\\beta n\\rfloor$, each is within $1/n$ of its target. A shift of $d$\nin one argument of $\\phi$ changes $\\phi$ by at most $\\phi(d)$ when $d\\le1/e$,\nand $\\phi(1/n)=(\\ln n)/n$; three shifts cost $3\\ln n$ in the exponent, which\nis the $3\\ln n$ of the theorem and the reason for $n\\ge3$. Likewise\n$t(t-1)\\ge a^{2}n^{2}-an$, and the $-an$ costs the term $c\\,a$. Multiplying\nthe two estimates of Step~3 into the bound of Step~2 gives\n$\\exp(nH(a,a,1-2a)-ca^{2}n+3\\ln n+ca)$, which is Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment};\n$a\\le1/2$ gives its second form. Nothing is hidden in an error term: the\ninequalities of Step~3 are exact bounds on the two factors, and the two\ncorrections are the ones just named~\\leanref{firstmomentA}.\n\n\\paragraph{The admissible region.}\nOn $(0,\\tfrac12]$ the function $g(a)=H(a,a,1-2a)-ca^{2}$ is strictly concave,\ntends to $0$ as $a\\to0$, and equals $\\ln2-c/4$ at $a=\\tfrac12$. If\n$c\\le4\\ln2$ it is nonnegative throughout, so $\\delta=-g$ is never positive.\nIf $c>4\\ln2$ it has exactly one zero $a^{*}$ in $(0,\\tfrac12)$, is positive\nbefore it and negative after it, so $\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$ exactly when\n$a=(1-\\beta)/2>a^{*}$, that is when $\\beta<1-2a^{*}=:\\beta^{*}(c)$.\nSubstituting $a=(1-\\beta)/2$ into $g(a)=0$ gives the equation of\nTheorem~\\ref{thm:betastar}, and the asymptotic form follows from the\ntwo-sided estimate $2\\ln(1/a)-ca\\le g(a)/a\\le2\\ln(1/a)+2-ca$. This part is\nproved on paper only.\n\n\\subsection{The benchmark generator}\n\\label{app:random-cs}\n\nThe random instances of Section~\\ref{sec:results} are not $G(n,p)$. The\ngenerator of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009} fills a customer-by-pattern table with\nindependent entries at a density chosen to give $d$ customers per pattern on\naverage, and discards any instance whose MOSP graph is disconnected; the\nfiles show two further rules the paper does not state, every customer given\nat least one pattern and every pattern at least two customers, and with those\nrules a simulated generator reproduces the size statistics of all forty\nclasses. The MOSP graph of such an instance is the union of one clique per\npattern. Steps 1 and 2 of the proof survive this change unaltered: a graph of\npathwidth $k$ still has an empty pair, and a pair is empty exactly when no\npattern contains a customer of each side. If the patterns are independent\nrandom customer sets of sizes $s_1,\\dots,s_m$, the probability that a given\npair $(A,R)$ with $|A|=t$, $|R|=t-1$ is empty factorises over the patterns,\nand the union bound gives\n\\[\n  \\Pr[\\mathrm{pw}\\le k]\\;\\le\\;\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}\\prod_{j=1}^{m}\n  \\frac{\\binom{n-t}{s_j}+\\binom{n-t+1}{s_j}-\\binom{n-2t+1}{s_j}}{\\binom{n}{s_j}},\n\\]\nthe fraction being the share of $s_j$-sets that miss $A$ or miss $R$. This is\na Lean theorem in the paper's own vocabulary, with the instance built from the\npatterns and the event stated as $\\mathrm{MOSP}\\le k+1$~\\leanref{clique-cover}.\nIt is computable from the pattern sizes alone, so it predicts the benchmark\nfrom the generator's parameters. Table~\\ref{tab:cs-floor} gives, for each of\nthe forty classes, the mean certified optimum of its five instances beside\nthe floor the inequality gives at confidence $0.01$, that is, the largest\nvalue it does not rule out at that level plus one. None of the 200 certified\noptima lies below its floor. At $d=10$ the floor is within $5\\%$ of the\noptimum, at $d=4$ within $20\\%$; at $d=2$ it is a third short, and\nasymptotically it says nothing there: with $m=\\mu n$ patterns of $d$\ncustomers, the exponent of the expected number of balanced empty pairs is\n$\\ln2\\,\\bigl(1+\\mu(1-d)\\bigr)$, so the method gives a positive constant\nexactly when $\\mu(d-1)>1$. That quantity is the mean number of patterns per\ncustomer minus one, the number of ones in the table minus the number of\npatterns, per customer; call it the excess. The classes of\nSection~\\ref{sec:results} that took thousands of core-hours to certify, the\nsparsest ones, have excess between $1.66$ and $1.78$, just inside the region\nwhere this argument proves linear pathwidth; on generated random covers the\ncost of the exact search peaks at excess about $2.3$ to $2.5$. Whether the\nthreshold and that peak are related is not known.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The Chu \\& Stuckey classes: mean certified optimum of the five\ninstances, and the floor from the first-moment inequality at confidence\n$0.01$, computed from the pattern sizes of each file. No certified optimum\nlies below its floor.}\n\\label{tab:cs-floor}\n\\small\n\\input{tables/cs_firstmoment}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe inequality assumes the patterns are uniform given their sizes, which the\npadding and the rejection break. What is proved is the inequality with the\nsizes the table has \\emph{before} the repairs. On the Bernoulli table a given\npair is empty with probability exactly\n$[(1-p)^t+(1-p)^{t-1}-(1-p)^{2t-1}]^m$, the binomial average of the fraction\nabove; the repairs only add ones, so an empty pair of the padded table is an\nempty pair of the original and the bound with Bernoulli sizes holds for the\npadded instance; and if the patterns are padded before the customers, the\nrows after that pad are independent uniform sets of size $\\max(s,2)$ with $s$\nbinomial, and the bound holds with those sizes. The rejection is not an\nincreasing event of the table entries: adding an entry can make a pad vanish,\ndisconnect the instance and lower its pathwidth, so Harris's inequality does\nnot apply to it, and conditioning on acceptance costs at most the factor\n$1/\\Pr[\\text{accepted}]$, which is measured: within $1\\%$ of $1$ at\n$d\\ge6$ and on the square classes at $d=4$, $0.77$ at the $100\\times50$,\n$d=4$ class, $0.53$ to $0.87$ at $d=2$, and $0.005$ at the $100\\times50$,\n$d=2$ class. A Chernoff bound on the pattern sizes gives a quenched form of\nthe same floor, two to four stacks higher on the largest classes. These\nproved floors lie below the floors of Table~\\ref{tab:cs-floor}, by zero to\nfive stacks at $d\\ge8$, by two to twelve at $d=4$ and $6$ on the square\nclasses and by more on the $100\\times50$ classes; at $d=2$ they are trivial,\nbecause $40\\%$ of the Bernoulli patterns have at most one customer and the\npads that make them count are exactly what the proof cannot use. What\nremains between the proved floors and the table is, at $d\\ge4$, the\ndifference between a bound on the average over size vectors and the typical\nsize vector, and at $d=2$ the pads. With the sizes the files actually have\nthe statement is measured, not proved: under the fitted generator the repairs and the\nrejection make empty pairs rarer than the uniform model says, by a factor\nthat falls from $1.0$ to $0.6$ as the sets grow, and on 7{,}200 fresh\ninstances at 30 and 40 customers solved exactly none has an optimum below its\n$0.01$ floor.\n\nThe same first moment appears in \\citet{Gao2012} for random intersection\ngraphs, with treewidth in place of pathwidth. His proof drops the factor\ncorresponding to the product above between two lines, which is harmless when\nthe number of patterns is sublinear in $n$ and changes the constant when it is\nlinear; his threshold of two patterns per customer is the dropped factor's\nartefact, and with it restored the condition at $m=n$ is about $2.46$.\n\n\\subsection{What is checked, and what is known}\n\nSteps 1, 2, 3 and 4 and the two inequalities of Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment}\nare Lean theorems with the statement exactly as printed, including the\nconstant~3; so is the consequence that the probability tends to zero whenever\n$\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$. The entropy bound on trinomial coefficients is not in\nMathlib and is proved there from the binomial theorem. Theorem~\\ref{thm:betastar}\nis proved on paper, and the values in Table~\\ref{tab:betastar} were computed\nby two programs written independently.\n\nThe argument is published twice in cruder form. \\citet{Turner1986} used the\nsame empty pair, with the count $(en/k)^{2k}$ in place of the entropy, to\nprove that almost every $G(n,p)$ with $p$ fixed has bandwidth at least\n$n-4\\log_{1/(1-p)}n$; since bandwidth is at least pathwidth, our dense\nstatement implies his and not conversely. \\citet{DiazPetitSernaTrevisan2001}\nused it for vertex separation on mixing graphs, with a $3^{n}$ count and\nChernoff slack at a single position, which gives no positive constant below\n$c\\approx227$. Neither states the entropy form, a value of $\\beta^{*}(c)$, the\nthreshold $4\\ln2$ or the asymptotics, and we found no later work that does.\n\nOne computation says how far the finite inequality is from the truth. On\n133 random graphs with 60 vertices and $c=10$ whose pathwidth we computed\nexactly, the smallest pathwidth is 25, while Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment}'s\nfirst inequality, evaluated exactly rather than through Step~3, puts the\nprobability of pathwidth at most 23 below $0.35$ and of at most 21 below\n$10^{-3}$. The gap between $\\beta^{*}(c)$ and the slopes fitted to such data\nis of the order of $0.1$ and does not close with $n$; closing it would need a\nsecond moment or a count over structured pairs, not a sharper estimate of\nthis one.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b64b2670-263a-4912-8a4a-d8fc3e1ebe26","uuid":"7289b92c-a8e9-42b3-b4aa-8031cffb4942","timestamp":"2026-11-01T07:49:49.934Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# The excess phase diagram of MOSP\n\n*Ralph loop0010, executing `reports/excess_phase_plan.md`. One section per\nitem; each states its size range, its regenerate command, its kill verdict\nand what it does not claim. The parameter throughout is the excess\n`e = (n_ones − m) / n`, the mean number of patterns per customer minus one,\nalways realised from the matrix and never the generator's nominal value.*\n\n----\n% Appendix E: the first-moment lower bound on the pathwidth of sparse random\n% graphs, in plain language (owner's request, 2026-10-31). Source:\n% reports/random_graph_first_moment.md (Theorems A, A', A'' and the literature\n% verdict); Lean: EmptyPair.lean, FirstMoment.lean, FirstMomentAsymptotics.lean.\n% The beta*(c) values: reports/scratch/first_moment/beta_star.log (two codes agree).\n\\section{The pathwidth of sparse random graphs}\n\\label{app:random}\n\nThe equivalences of Section~\\ref{sec:equiv} make every statement about the\npathwidth of a graph a statement about the open stacks of an instance. This\nappendix proves one such statement for random graphs, in plain language, and\nrecords what of it is checked in Lean. The result is that a sparse random\ngraph has pathwidth proportional to its number of vertices, with an explicit\nconstant. The argument is a first moment, an old tool; what is new here is the\nexact form of its deterministic step, the constant it yields, and the\nmachine check. We found no prior statement of the constant.\n\n\\subsection{The statement}\n\n$G(n,p)$ is the random graph on $n$ labelled vertices in which each of the\n$\\binom n2$ possible edges is present with probability $p$, independently. We\ntake $p=c/n$, so that a vertex has about $c$ neighbours. Write\n$\\mathrm{pw}$ for pathwidth, $\\ln$ for the natural logarithm, and\n\\[\n  \\phi(x) = -x\\ln x \\quad(\\phi(0)=0),\\qquad\n  H(x,y,z) = \\phi(x)+\\phi(y)+\\phi(z)\n\\]\nfor the entropy of a probability vector with three entries.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[first moment, finite form]\n\\label{thm:firstmoment}\nLet $c>0$ and $0<\\beta<1$, and put $a=(1-\\beta)/2$ and\n\\[\n  \\delta(\\beta,c) \\;=\\; c\\,a^{2} \\;-\\; H(a,a,1-2a).\n\\]\nFor every integer $n\\ge\\max(3,c)$,\n\\[\n  \\Pr\\bigl[\\mathrm{pw}(G(n,c/n))\\le\\beta n\\bigr]\n  \\;\\le\\; \\exp\\bigl(-\\delta(\\beta,c)\\,n+3\\ln n+c\\,a\\bigr)\n  \\;\\le\\; e^{c/2}\\,n^{3}\\,e^{-\\delta(\\beta,c)\\,n}.\n\\]\n\\end{theorem}\n\nSo whenever $\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$, the probability that the pathwidth is as small\nas $\\beta n$ is exponentially small, and the pathwidth is at least $\\beta n$\nwith high probability. The next statement says for which $\\beta$ that happens.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the admissible region]\n\\label{thm:betastar}\nIf $c\\le4\\ln2$ there is no $\\beta\\in(0,1)$ with $\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$. If\n$c>4\\ln2$ there is a number $\\beta^{*}(c)\\in(0,1)$ such that\n$\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$ exactly when $\\beta<\\beta^{*}(c)$; it is the root in\n$(0,1)$ of\n\\[\n  -(1-\\beta)\\ln\\tfrac{1-\\beta}{2}-\\beta\\ln\\beta \\;=\\; \\tfrac{c}{4}(1-\\beta)^{2}.\n\\]\nHence $\\mathrm{pw}(G(n,c/n))\\ge\\beta n$ with probability $1-O(n^{3}e^{-\\delta n})$\nfor every $\\beta<\\beta^{*}(c)$, and as $c\\to\\infty$,\n$1-\\beta^{*}(c)=\\bigl(4\\ln c-4\\ln\\ln c+O(1)\\bigr)/c$.\n\\end{theorem}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:betastar} gives $\\beta^{*}(c)$ at a few values of $c$, beside the\nonly published explicit lower bound we know for a width parameter of\n$G(n,c/n)$, the treewidth bound $1-\\sqrt{9\\ln3/(2c)}$ of\n\\citet{PerarnauSerra2014}, which is a lower bound on pathwidth too since\ntreewidth never exceeds pathwidth.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The constant $\\beta^{*}(c)$ of Theorem~\\ref{thm:betastar}, to four\ndecimals, and the treewidth bound of \\citet{PerarnauSerra2014} at the same\n$c$ (blank where that bound is not positive). Two independent programs agree\non every digit of $\\beta^{*}$.}\n\\label{tab:betastar}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrrr@{}}\n\\toprule\n$c$ & 3 & 4 & 6 & 8 & 10 & 20 & 50 & 100 \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\beta^{*}(c)$ & 0.0087 & 0.0613 & 0.1706 & 0.2628 & 0.3371 & 0.5535 & 0.7606 & 0.8574 \\\\\n$1-\\sqrt{9\\ln3/(2c)}$ & & & 0.0918 & 0.2136 & 0.2966 & 0.5027 & 0.6855 & 0.7776 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe threshold $4\\ln2\\approx2.77$ in Theorem~\\ref{thm:betastar} belongs to the\nmethod, not to the graphs: it is the point at which an edge-free split of the\nvertices into two equal halves stops being likely, and below it the first\nmoment simply says nothing. The pathwidth of $G(n,c/n)$ is in fact linear in\n$n$ for every $c>1$ \\citep{LeeLeeOum2012}, with a constant that no one has\nmade explicit.\n\n\\subsection{The proof, in four steps}\n\n\\paragraph{Step 1: a narrow layout leaves an empty pair.}\nPut the vertices in a line and look at any position $i$. The first $i+1$\nvertices are the prefix; the rest are the suffix. A suffix vertex is\n\\emph{active} if it has a neighbour in the prefix. The vertex separation of\nthe line is the largest number of active vertices at any position, and by\nTheorem~\\ref{thm:vs} the smallest vertex separation over all lines is the\npathwidth. Now take a line of vertex separation $k$ and any position $i$. The\nsuffix has $n-i-1$ vertices, at most $k$ of them active, so at least $n-i-1-k$\nsuffix vertices have no neighbour at all in the prefix. Call that set $R$ and\nthe prefix $A$. Then $A$ and $R$ are disjoint, $|A|=i+1$, $|R|\\ge n-i-1-k$, and\nthere is no edge between them: an \\emph{empty pair}. This holds at every\nposition of every line, so a graph of pathwidth $k$ has an empty pair with\n$|A|=t$ and $|R|\\ge n-t-k$ for every $t\\le n$. With $t=\\lceil(n-k)/2\\rceil$\nboth parts have about $(n-k)/2$ vertices~\\leanref{emptypair}.\n\n\\paragraph{Step 2: count the graphs that have one.}\nFix a pair of disjoint sets $A$ and $R$ with $|A|=t$ and $|R|=t-1$. In\n$G(n,p)$ the $t(t-1)$ possible edges between them are all absent with\nprobability $(1-p)^{t(t-1)}$. There are $\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}$ such\npairs. A graph of pathwidth at most $k$ has at least one of them empty, so by\nthe union bound\n\\[\n  \\Pr[\\mathrm{pw}\\le k]\\;\\le\\;\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}\\,(1-p)^{t(t-1)},\n  \\qquad t=\\lceil(n-k)/2\\rceil .\n\\]\nThis is the whole probabilistic content of the proof, and it is a Lean\ntheorem over the finite sum of the weights $p^{|E|}(1-p)^{\\binom n2-|E|}$ of\nall graphs on $n$ vertices, with no probability model needed; the count of\ngraphs with a given empty pair is exact there, $2^{\\binom n2-t(t-1)}$ of\nthem~\\leanref{firstmoment}.\n\n\\paragraph{Step 3: estimate the count.}\nTwo standard inequalities turn the right-hand side into an exponential in\n$n$. The number of ways to choose the pair is the trinomial coefficient\n$n!/\\bigl(t!\\,(t-1)!\\,(n-2t+1)!\\bigr)$, and a trinomial coefficient is at most\n$\\exp(nH)$ with $H$ the entropy of the three proportions $t/n$, $(t-1)/n$ and\n$(n-2t+1)/n$ \\citep[Theorem~11.1.3]{CoverThomas2006}: expand\n$(x+y+z)^{n}=1$ at $x=t/n$, $y=(t-1)/n$, $z=(n-2t+1)/n$ and keep one term of\nthe sum. And $(1-p)^{m}\\le e^{-pm}$ because $1-p\\le e^{-p}$. With $p=c/n$ the\nsecond factor is at most $\\exp(-c\\,t(t-1)/n)$.\n\n\\paragraph{Step 4: the bookkeeping.}\nThe three proportions are not exactly $a$, $a$ and $1-2a$: with\n$k=\\lfloor\\beta n\\rfloor$, each is within $1/n$ of its target. A shift of $d$\nin one argument of $\\phi$ changes $\\phi$ by at most $\\phi(d)$ when $d\\le1/e$,\nand $\\phi(1/n)=(\\ln n)/n$; three shifts cost $3\\ln n$ in the exponent, which\nis the $3\\ln n$ of the theorem and the reason for $n\\ge3$. Likewise\n$t(t-1)\\ge a^{2}n^{2}-an$, and the $-an$ costs the term $c\\,a$. Multiplying\nthe two estimates of Step~3 into the bound of Step~2 gives\n$\\exp(nH(a,a,1-2a)-ca^{2}n+3\\ln n+ca)$, which is Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment};\n$a\\le1/2$ gives its second form. Nothing is hidden in an error term: the\ninequalities of Step~3 are exact bounds on the two factors, and the two\ncorrections are the ones just named~\\leanref{firstmomentA}.\n\n\\paragraph{The admissible region.}\nOn $(0,\\tfrac12]$ the function $g(a)=H(a,a,1-2a)-ca^{2}$ is strictly concave,\ntends to $0$ as $a\\to0$, and equals $\\ln2-c/4$ at $a=\\tfrac12$. If\n$c\\le4\\ln2$ it is nonnegative throughout, so $\\delta=-g$ is never positive.\nIf $c>4\\ln2$ it has exactly one zero $a^{*}$ in $(0,\\tfrac12)$, is positive\nbefore it and negative after it, so $\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$ exactly when\n$a=(1-\\beta)/2>a^{*}$, that is when $\\beta<1-2a^{*}=:\\beta^{*}(c)$.\nSubstituting $a=(1-\\beta)/2$ into $g(a)=0$ gives the equation of\nTheorem~\\ref{thm:betastar}, and the asymptotic form follows from the\ntwo-sided estimate $2\\ln(1/a)-ca\\le g(a)/a\\le2\\ln(1/a)+2-ca$. This part is\nproved on paper only.\n\n\\subsection{The benchmark generator}\n\\label{app:random-cs}\n\nThe random instances of Section~\\ref{sec:results} are not $G(n,p)$. The\ngenerator of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009} fills a customer-by-pattern table with\nindependent entries at a density chosen to give $d$ customers per pattern on\naverage, and discards any instance whose MOSP graph is disconnected; the\nfiles show two further rules the paper does not state, every customer given\nat least one pattern and every pattern at least two customers, and with those\nrules a simulated generator reproduces the size statistics of all forty\nclasses. The MOSP graph of such an instance is the union of one clique per\npattern. Steps 1 and 2 of the proof survive this change unaltered: a graph of\npathwidth $k$ still has an empty pair, and a pair is empty exactly when no\npattern contains a customer of each side. If the patterns are independent\nrandom customer sets of sizes $s_1,\\dots,s_m$, the probability that a given\npair $(A,R)$ with $|A|=t$, $|R|=t-1$ is empty factorises over the patterns,\nand the union bound gives\n\\[\n  \\Pr[\\mathrm{pw}\\le k]\\;\\le\\;\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}\\prod_{j=1}^{m}\n  \\frac{\\binom{n-t}{s_j}+\\binom{n-t+1}{s_j}-\\binom{n-2t+1}{s_j}}{\\binom{n}{s_j}},\n\\]\nthe fraction being the share of $s_j$-sets that miss $A$ or miss $R$. This is\na Lean theorem in the paper's own vocabulary, with the instance built from the\npatterns and the event stated as $\\mathrm{MOSP}\\le k+1$~\\leanref{clique-cover}.\nIt is computable from the pattern sizes alone, so it predicts the benchmark\nfrom the generator's parameters. Table~\\ref{tab:cs-floor} gives, for each of\nthe forty classes, the mean certified optimum of its five instances beside\nthe floor the inequality gives at confidence $0.01$, that is, the largest\nvalue it does not rule out at that level plus one. None of the 200 certified\noptima lies below its floor. At $d=10$ the floor is within $5\\%$ of the\noptimum, at $d=4$ within $20\\%$; at $d=2$ it is a third short, and\nasymptotically it says nothing there: with $m=\\mu n$ patterns of $d$\ncustomers, the exponent of the expected number of balanced empty pairs is\n$\\ln2\\,\\bigl(1+\\mu(1-d)\\bigr)$, so the method gives a positive constant\nexactly when $\\mu(d-1)>1$. That quantity is the mean number of patterns per\ncustomer minus one, the number of ones in the table minus the number of\npatterns, per customer; call it the excess. The classes of\nSection~\\ref{sec:results} that took thousands of core-hours to certify, the\nsparsest ones, have excess between $1.66$ and $1.78$, just inside the region\nwhere this argument proves linear pathwidth; on generated random covers the\ncost of the exact search peaks at excess about $2.3$ to $2.5$. Whether the\nthreshold and that peak are related is not known.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The Chu \\& Stuckey classes: mean certified optimum of the five\ninstances, and the floor from the first-moment inequality at confidence\n$0.01$, computed from the pattern sizes of each file. No certified optimum\nlies below its floor.}\n\\label{tab:cs-floor}\n\\small\n\\input{tables/cs_firstmoment}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe inequality assumes the patterns are uniform given their sizes, which the\npadding and the rejection break. What is proved is the inequality with the\nsizes the table has \\emph{before} the repairs. On the Bernoulli table a given\npair is empty with probability exactly\n$[(1-p)^t+(1-p)^{t-1}-(1-p)^{2t-1}]^m$, the binomial average of the fraction\nabove; the repairs only add ones, so an empty pair of the padded table is an\nempty pair of the original and the bound with Bernoulli sizes holds for the\npadded instance; and if the patterns are padded before the customers, the\nrows after that pad are independent uniform sets of size $\\max(s,2)$ with $s$\nbinomial, and the bound holds with those sizes. The rejection is not an\nincreasing event of the table entries: adding an entry can make a pad vanish,\ndisconnect the instance and lower its pathwidth, so Harris's inequality does\nnot apply to it, and conditioning on acceptance costs at most the factor\n$1/\\Pr[\\text{accepted}]$, which is measured: within $1\\%$ of $1$ at\n$d\\ge6$ and on the square classes at $d=4$, $0.77$ at the $100\\times50$,\n$d=4$ class, $0.53$ to $0.87$ at $d=2$, and $0.005$ at the $100\\times50$,\n$d=2$ class. A Chernoff bound on the pattern sizes gives a quenched form of\nthe same floor, two to four stacks higher on the largest classes. These\nproved floors lie below the floors of Table~\\ref{tab:cs-floor}, by zero to\nfive stacks at $d\\ge8$, by two to twelve at $d=4$ and $6$ on the square\nclasses and by more on the $100\\times50$ classes; at $d=2$ they are trivial,\nbecause $40\\%$ of the Bernoulli patterns have at most one customer and the\npads that make them count are exactly what the proof cannot use. What\nremains between the proved floors and the table is, at $d\\ge4$, the\ndifference between a bound on the average over size vectors and the typical\nsize vector, and at $d=2$ the pads. With the sizes the files actually have\nthe statement is measured, not proved: under the fitted generator the repairs and the\nrejection make empty pairs rarer than the uniform model says, by a factor\nthat falls from $1.0$ to $0.6$ as the sets grow, and on 7{,}200 fresh\ninstances at 30 and 40 customers solved exactly none has an optimum below its\n$0.01$ floor.\n\nThe same first moment appears in \\citet{Gao2012} for random intersection\ngraphs, with treewidth in place of pathwidth. His proof drops the factor\ncorresponding to the product above between two lines, which is harmless when\nthe number of patterns is sublinear in $n$ and changes the constant when it is\nlinear; his threshold of two patterns per customer is the dropped factor's\nartefact, and with it restored the condition at $m=n$ is about $2.46$.\n\n\\subsection{What is checked, and what is known}\n\nSteps 1, 2, 3 and 4 and the two inequalities of Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment}\nare Lean theorems with the statement exactly as printed, including the\nconstant~3; so is the consequence that the probability tends to zero whenever\n$\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$. The entropy bound on trinomial coefficients is not in\nMathlib and is proved there from the binomial theorem. Theorem~\\ref{thm:betastar}\nis proved on paper, and the values in Table~\\ref{tab:betastar} were computed\nby two programs written independently.\n\nThe argument is published twice in cruder form. \\citet{Turner1986} used the\nsame empty pair, with the count $(en/k)^{2k}$ in place of the entropy, to\nprove that almost every $G(n,p)$ with $p$ fixed has bandwidth at least\n$n-4\\log_{1/(1-p)}n$; since bandwidth is at least pathwidth, our dense\nstatement implies his and not conversely. \\citet{DiazPetitSernaTrevisan2001}\nused it for vertex separation on mixing graphs, with a $3^{n}$ count and\nChernoff slack at a single position, which gives no positive constant below\n$c\\approx227$. Neither states the entropy form, a value of $\\beta^{*}(c)$, the\nthreshold $4\\ln2$ or the asymptotics, and we found no later work that does.\n\nOne computation says how far the finite inequality is from the truth. On\n133 random graphs with 60 vertices and $c=10$ whose pathwidth we computed\nexactly, the smallest pathwidth is 25, while Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment}'s\nfirst inequality, evaluated exactly rather than through Step~3, puts the\nprobability of pathwidth at most 23 below $0.35$ and of at most 21 below\n$10^{-3}$. The gap between $\\beta^{*}(c)$ and the slopes fitted to such data\nis of the order of $0.1$ and does not close with $n$; closing it would need a\nsecond moment or a count over structured pairs, not a sharper estimate of\nthis one.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01JTg2hqMfRUtR8GkQspAZrA","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011Cgbqn3W8ip2mySrRYA7Y5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018LKwKBCzH2hztHwQQjQDdQ","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat paper1/latex/sec9a_appendix_lean.tex && echo ---- && cat paper1/latex/main.tex","description":"Read the Lean appendix and main.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3304,"cache_read_input_tokens":98612,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3304,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b64b2670-263a-4912-8a4a-d8fc3e1ebe26","uuid":"760ac2f9-743f-4da3-8645-05de0a0015cb","timestamp":"2026-11-01T07:49:51.099Z","request_id":"req_011Cgbqn374zpdn5yx9yUDPJ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_018LKwKBCzH2hztHwQQjQDdQ":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP && cat paper1/latex/sec9a_appendix_lean.tex && echo ---- && cat paper1/latex/main.tex","description":"Read the Lean appendix and main.tex"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_018LKwKBCzH2hztHwQQjQDdQ":{"cwd":"/home/al/dev/MOSP"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018LKwKBCzH2hztHwQQjQDdQ","type":"tool_result","content":"% Appendix A: the formal names (loop0009 item 01). Every Lean-name footnote of\n% the draft moved here; the text cites an entry as (Appendix A, A.n) with\n% the leanref macro. check_lean.py and check_axioms.py read the leannames\n% cells. Axiom line printed by paper1/axiom_check.lean (`make axioms`).\n\\section{Formal names}\n\\label{app:lean}\n\nEvery theorem, lemma and counterexample of this paper is checked in the Lean~4\nproof assistant with the Mathlib library, unless the text says otherwise. The\nreader need not read Lean to follow the paper. This appendix is for a reader\nwho wants to find the formal statement of a result: Table~\\ref{tab:lean} maps\neach result, in the order in which it appears, to the names of the Lean\ndeclarations that state it. Where a row cites a definition rather than a\ntheorem, it is the definition the text uses. Lean names use \\texttt{repaired}\nfor what the text calls \\emph{corrected}.\n\nAll the theorems named depend only on the three standard axioms of Lean's\nclassical logic, \\texttt{propext}, \\texttt{Classical.choice} and\n\\texttt{Quot.sound}; an axiom check in the repository prints this for every\ntheorem named in the paper. The development has no \\texttt{sorry}, Lean's marker for a statement left\nunproved.\n\nTwo Lean proofs take a route that the text does not describe. The proof of\nTheorem~\\ref{thm:decides} goes through narrowness \\citep{LY2002ref11}, which\ncounts the closing order forwards rather than reversing it. The proof of\nTheorem~\\ref{thm:matching} reduces the deficiency form of Hall's theorem to\nMathlib's Hall theorem by adding $\\delta$ dummy stacks. One Lean proof takes a\ndifferent route from the published one: the proof\nthat node search is monotone (Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(5)) follows the crusade\nargument of \\citet{BienstockSeymour1991}, not the reduction to edge search of\n\\citet{LaPaugh1993} on which \\citet{LY2002ref10} rely. Three kinds of fact\nare computed rather than proved. The values of $K_2$, $K_{1,3}$ and $K_{3,3}$\nafter Theorem~\\ref{thm:bands}, which show that every end of the two bands\noccurs, are computed by brute force from each quantity's own definition. The\nsearch for smaller counterexamples (Appendix~\\ref{app:minimality}) and the\nresults of Section~\\ref{sec:results} are computations.\n\n{\\small\n\\begin{longtable}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{1.0cm}>{\\raggedright}p{6.6cm}>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{8.0cm}@{}}\n\\caption{Every result of the paper has a formal statement in Lean, listed\nhere in the order in which the results appear.}\\label{tab:lean}\\\\\n\\toprule\nEntry & Result in the paper & Lean names \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\endfirsthead\n\\toprule\nEntry & Result in the paper & Lean names \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\endhead\n\\bottomrule\n\\endfoot\n\\leangroup{Section~\\ref{sec:equiv}, the equivalences}\n\\leanentry{clique}{Lemma~\\ref{lem:clique}, cliques sit in a bag} \\leannames{PathDecomposition.exists_bag_of_isClique} \\\\\n\\leanentry{vs}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:vs}, vertex separation is pathwidth} \\leannames{vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth} \\\\\n\\leanentry{mosp}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp}, MOSP is pathwidth plus one} \\leannames{MOSPInstance.mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{star}{The pattern graph gives no bound: the star $K_{1,4}$ (after Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp})} \\leannames{star_mospValue, star_pathwidth_agreementGraph, star_refutes_pattern_graph_bound} \\\\\n\\leanentry{core-gml}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(1), gate matrix layout} \\leannames{NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_mospValue, NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{core-it}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(2), interval thickness} \\leannames{intervalThickness_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{core-logic}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(3), one-dimensional logic} \\leannames{LogicArray.tracks_eq_intervalThickness, LogicArray.tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{core-narrow}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(4), narrowness} \\leannames{narrowness_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{core-ns}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(5), node search} \\leannames{nodeSearchMonotonicity, nodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one, nodeSearch_chain} \\\\\n\\leanentry{core-fold}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(6), multiple folding} \\leannames{NetGateMatrix.foldTracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{core-vsg}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(7), Lengauer's vertex separator game} \\leannames{vsg_eq_max} \\\\\n\\leanentry{bands}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:bands}, the bands} \\leannames{pathwidth_le_splitBandwidth_le_pathwidth_add_one, vertexSeparation_le_edgeSearch_le_add_two, vertexSeparation_le_progressiveEdgeSearch_le_add_two} \\\\\n\\leanentry{false-pla}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:false}(1), PLA folding} \\leannames{plaTracks_idMatrix_five, plaTracks_idMatrix_unbounded, plaTracks_pathMatrix_unbounded} \\\\\n\\leanentry{false-cw}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:false}(2), edge separation read as cutwidth} \\leannames{cutwidth_unbounded, modCutwidth_unbounded} \\\\\n\\leangroup{Section~\\ref{sec:search}, the search}\n\\leanentry{defs}{Step cost, $\\Ext_k$ and $\\Sol_k$ (Section~\\ref{sec:search-def})} \\leannames{stepCost, Solvable, SearchSol} \\\\\n\\leanentry{F}{Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}, free moves never hurt} \\leannames{solvable_iff_searchSol_cl} \\\\\n\\leanentry{decides}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:decides}, the search decides pathwidth} \\leannames{searchSol_empty_iff_pathwidth_add_one_le, searchSol_mospGraph_iff_mospValue_le} \\\\\n\\leanentry{dict1}{Table~\\ref{tab:dictionary}: closing order from $\\emptyset$} \\leannames{orderOfLayout} \\\\\n\\leanentry{dict2}{Table~\\ref{tab:dictionary}: open stacks $b(T)$} \\leannames{openStacks_eq_card_boundary} \\\\\n\\leanentry{dict3}{Table~\\ref{tab:dictionary}: $\\cost(T,c)$} \\leannames{stepCost_eq_card_boundary_add_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{dict4}{Table~\\ref{tab:dictionary}: $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$} \\leannames{searchSol_empty_iff_pathwidth_add_one_le} \\\\\n\\leanentry{dict5}{Table~\\ref{tab:dictionary}: free closure $\\cl(T)$} \\leannames{mem_cl_iff} \\\\\n\\leanentry{dict6}{Table~\\ref{tab:dictionary}: definite move, corrected} \\leannames{isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_isCommittable} \\\\\n\\leanentry{premise}{Lemma~\\ref{lem:premise}, what the premise says} \\leannames{closeCount_eq, isDefinite_iff} \\\\\n\\leanentry{cex}{Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite}} \\leannames{definiteMove_counterexample, cexFamily_closed} \\\\\n\\leanentry{thm1false}{Chu and Stuckey's Theorem~1 (the definite move), under both readings, and Chu's Theorem~6.3.6 are false} \\leannames{chuStuckey_theorem1_false, chuStuckey_theorem1_false_literal, chuThesis_theorem636_false} \\\\\n\\leanentry{fink}{Fink's restatement of the definite move is false} \\leannames{fink_theorem1_false} \\\\\n\\leanentry{hered}{The hereditarily definite premise, and its failure in Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite}} \\leannames{IsHereditarilyDefinite, not_isHereditarilyDefinite_cex} \\\\\n\\leanentry{submod}{Lemma~\\ref{lem:submod}, open stacks are submodular} \\leannames{openStacks_submodular} \\\\\n\\leanentry{repaired}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:repaired}, the corrected definite move is sound} \\leannames{solvable_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite, searchSol_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite} \\\\\n\\leanentry{matching}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:matching}, the corrected premise is a matching condition} \\leannames{isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_hasDefiniteMatching} \\\\\n\\leanentry{tamaki}{Lemma~1 of \\citet{Kitsunai2016}, transcribed for digraphs in their terms and proved by their argument; on the symmetric digraph with the extension fixed to the child, its commitment condition is the hereditary premise, our theorem follows from it under its two feasibility hypotheses, it follows from ours, and a five-vertex graph shows those hypotheses are not redundant} \\leannames{Tamaki.lemma1, Tamaki.inDeg_submodular, Tamaki.isCommittableExt_symm_iff_hereditarilyDefinite, Tamaki.solvable_cl_insert_of_lemma1, Tamaki.lemma1_symm_of_isCommittable, Tamaki.premise_gap} \\\\\n\\leanentry{open1}{The published definite move is right when $\\open(q,S)\\le1$} \\leannames{isHereditarilyDefinite_of_openCount_le_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{subset}{The subset rule is sound} \\leannames{searchSol_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset, subsetFilter_sound} \\\\\n\\leanentry{tiebreak}{The subset rule needs its tie-break (7-customer graph)} \\leannames{noTieBreak_counterexample} \\\\\n\\leanentry{isbetter}{The better move's premise, restated} \\leannames{isBetter_iff} \\\\\n\\leanentry{bettercex}{Chu and Stuckey's Theorem~2 (the better move) is false} \\leannames{betterMove_counterexample, chuStuckey_theorem2_false} \\\\\n\\leanentry{thm638}{Chu's Theorem~6.3.8 is false} \\leannames{chuThesis_theorem638_false, chuThesis_theorem638_false_literal} \\\\\n\\leanentry{repbetter}{The corrected better move is sound} \\leannames{IsRepairedBetter, searchSol_cl_insert_of_repairedBetter} \\\\\n\\leanentry{bugs}{The better move's two requirements: the close count and the rule order} \\leannames{bugA_counterexample, bugB_counterexample} \\\\\n\\leanentry{oldmove}{The old move is sound} \\leannames{searchSol_reinsert, not_searchSol_of_oldMove} \\\\\n\\leanentry{reinsert}{The old move needs its reinsertion test (path on five vertices)} \\leannames{reinsert_needs_test} \\\\\n\\leanentry{memo}{Solvability of a state does not depend on the path to it (the memo)} \\leannames{solvable_iff_of_cl_eq} \\\\\n\\leanentry{fakeold}{A node with an unrefuted old move can record a solvable state} \\leannames{exec_fake_oldMove} \\\\\n\\leanentry{nodesound}{Node-soundness of a filter (Section~\\ref{sec:theorem})} \\leannames{NodeSoundAt} \\\\\n\\leanentry{main}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:main}, our corrected search is sound} \\leannames{exec_repairedFullFilter_sound, exec_repairedFullFilter_pathwidth, exec_repairedFullFilter_mospValue} \\\\\n\\leanentry{notsound}{Under the previous algorithm the filter is not node-sound} \\leannames{not_codeFilterSound_cexGraph} \\\\\n\\leanentry{wrong}{Figure~\\ref{fig:wrong}: the previous algorithm refutes 6 and finds 7 on the 34-customer instance, whose optimum is 6 and pathwidth 5; its run is a run of the search with the published filter, which is therefore not sound there} \\leannames{previousAlgorithm_wrong_optimum, not_filterSound_codeFilter} \\\\\n\\leanentry{minor}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:minor}: contracting an edge (merging $v$ into $u$, as the solvers do) or deleting a vertex or an edge never increases pathwidth; along a legal contraction sequence, a minor of pathwidth above $w$ forces pathwidth above $w$, and on the MOSP graph more than $w+1$ open stacks} \\leannames{pathwidth_contractEdge_le, pathwidth_induce_le, pathwidth_mono, pathwidth_minorOf_le, pathwidth_gt_of_minorOf, MOSPInstance.lt_mospValue_of_minorOf} \\\\\n\\leanentry{grid}{The $(h,w)$-grid has pathwidth $\\min(h,w)$, except the $1\\times1$ grid (pathwidth $0$); \\citet[Theorem~4.1]{EllisWarren2008}. Upper bound by the row-major layout's bandwidth; lower bound directly on vertex separation, at the first prefix that completes a row or a column} \\leannames{pathwidth_grid, pathwidth_grid_le, min_le_pathwidth_grid, pathwidth_grid_one_one, pathwidth_grid_square} \\\\\n\\leanentry{hypercube}{The $d$-dimensional hypercube has pathwidth $\\sum_{i<d}\\binom{i}{\\lfloor i/2\\rfloor}$ \\citep[Theorem~2]{ChandranKavitha2006}, with Harper's vertex-isoperimetric theorem \\citep{Harper1966} proved by compression. Upper bound by the layout along Harper's simplicial order, lower bound at a prefix of every size, both by induction on $d$ and on vertex separation} \\leannames{pathwidth_hypercube, pathwidth_cubeGraph, Hypercube.harper, Hypercube.card_nbhd_init_le, Hypercube.exists_init_profile} \\\\\n\\leanentry{mycielski}{For every graph $G$, its Mycielskian $\\mu(G)$ (originals, a shadow of each vertex adjacent to its neighbours, and an apex adjacent to the shadows) has $\\mathrm{pw}(G)+1\\le\\mathrm{pw}(\\mu(G))\\le 2\\,\\mathrm{pw}(G)+1$. The upper bound interleaves each vertex with its shadow and places the apex last; we found no published statement of it. The lower bound keeps the bags meeting the apex and the shadows} \\leannames{pathwidth_mycielskian_le, pathwidth_add_one_le_mycielskian} \\\\\n\\leanentry{emptypair}{Step~1 of Appendix~\\ref{app:random}: at every position of every layout the prefix and the inactive suffix are an empty pair, so for every $j\\le n$ some $j$-set has closed neighbourhood of size at most $j+\\pw$ (Harper's bound in layout form), and $n-2s^{*}-2\\le\\pw$ for the empty-pair index $s^{*}$} \\leannames{exists_empty_pair_of_layout, exists_closedNbhd_card_le, exists_empty_pair, card_sub_two_mul_emptyPairIndex_sub_two_le_pathwidth} \\\\\n\\leanentry{firstmoment}{Step~2 of Appendix~\\ref{app:random}: the number of graphs on $n$ labelled vertices of pathwidth at most $k$ is at most $\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}2^{\\binom n2-t(t-1)}$, $t=\\lceil(n-k)/2\\rceil$, and the $G(n,p)$-weighted sum of those graphs is at most $\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}(1-p)^{t(t-1)}$; there are $2^{\\binom n2}$ graphs on $n$ labelled vertices} \\leannames{card_pathwidth_le_le, weightedSum_graphsOfPathwidthLe_le, card_emptyPairGraphs, card_simpleGraph} \\\\\n\\leanentry{firstmomentA}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment} and its consequence: the weighted sum is at most $\\exp(-\\delta n+3\\ln n+ca)$ for $n\\ge\\max(3,c)$, at most $e^{c/2}n^{3}e^{-\\delta n}$, and tends to $0$ when $\\delta>0$; the trinomial entropy bound from the binomial theorem} \\leannames{firstMoment_pathwidth_le_fin, firstMoment_pathwidth_le_fin', tendsto_firstMoment_pathwidth, choose_mul_choose_le_exp_entropy} \\\\\n\\leanentry{clique-cover}{Appendix~\\ref{app:random-cs}: for independent uniform patterns of given sizes, the probability that the instance's MOSP value is at most $k+1$ (its pathwidth at most $k$) is at most $\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}\\prod_j N(s_j)/\\binom n{s_j}$, $N(s)$ the number of $s$-sets missing $A$ or missing $R$} \\leannames{prob_coversOfMospValueLe_le, prob_coversOfPathwidthLe_le, card_coversOfPathwidthLe_le, card_filter_avoid} \\\\\n\\leanentry{endpoint}{Lemma~\\ref{lem:endpoint}, the premises as commitment conditions} \\leannames{isDefinite_iff_endpoint, isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_isCommittable} \\\\\n\\leanentry{cexnotcommit}{In Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} the endpoint test passes and the commitment fails} \\leannames{cex_isDefinite_not_isCommittable} \\\\\n\\leanentry{existscommit}{The published premise gives a commitment to the least-border set} \\leannames{exists_isCommittable_of_isDefinite} \\\\\n\\leanentry{commit234}{In Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} that set is $\\{2,3,4\\}$} \\leannames{cex_isCommittable_234} \\\\\n\\leanentry{depth1}{A commitment of depth one holds iff $\\open(q,S)\\le1$} \\leannames{isCommittable_insert_iff} \\\\\n\\leanentry{greedy}{The greedy step of Coudert, Mazauric and Nisse is that condition} \\leannames{isGreedyStep_iff} \\\\\n\\leangroup{Section~\\ref{sec:results}, computational results}\n\\leanentry{split}{The split of the root into independent tasks is sound} \\leannames{execSplit_repairedFullFilter_sound, execSplit_repairedFullFilter_mospValue} \\\\\n\\leangroup{Appendix~\\ref{app:machinery-all}, supporting material}\n\\leanentry{edgeless-ns}{Node search equalities need an edge (Appendix~\\ref{app:edge})} \\leannames{nodeSearch_ne_intervalThickness_of_edgeless, monotoneNodeSearch_ne_vertexSeparation_add_one_of_edgeless} \\\\\n\\leanentry{vsg0}{Lengauer's Theorem~4 fails at $k=0$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:edge})} \\leannames{isPositiveVSG_triangleGraph_counterexample} \\\\\n\\leanentry{tns}{$t=\\ns$ fails on the matrix $[1]$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:edge})} \\leannames{tracks_ne_monotoneNodeSearch_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{kp}{The Kirousis--Papadimitriou claim fails on $K_{1,3}$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:edge})} \\leannames{kirousisPapadimitriou_claim2_false} \\\\\n\\leanentry{pebbling}{Progressive pebbling is in the exact core (Appendix~\\ref{app:thirteenth})} \\leannames{pbw_lengauerD_eq_pathwidth, pbw_eq_mospValue_pebbleMatrix, mpb_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{vsearch}{The vertex search is in the exact core (Appendix~\\ref{app:thirteenth})} \\leannames{vertexSearchNumber_eq_pathwidth_add_one, monotoneVertexSearchNumber_eq_pathwidth_add_one, isNodeSearch_of_isSuccessfulVertexSearch, vertexSearchNumber_eq_nodeSearch, vertexSearchNumber_of_isEmpty} \\\\\n\\leanentry{wavefront}{The maximum wavefront is in the exact core (Appendix~\\ref{app:thirteenth})} \\leannames{card_wavefront, maxWavefront_eq_vertexSepOfLayout_add_one, minMaxWavefront_eq_pathwidth_add_one, minMaxWavefront_eq_narrowness, minMaxWavefront_of_isEmpty} \\\\\n\\leanentry{filter}{Proposition~\\ref{prop:filter}, the corrected filter is node-sound} \\leannames{repairedFullFilter_sound} \\\\\n\\leanentry{runs}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:runs}, runs are sound} \\leannames{Exec.sound} \\\\\n\\leanentry{encoding}{Appendix~\\ref{app:sat}, the SAT formulation is faithful (clauses 1--4)} \\leannames{encodes_iff_mospValue_le} \\\\\n\\end{longtable}\n}\n----\n% The pathwidth complex -- first full draft (Ralph loop0008 item 08, 2026-10-03).\n%\n% Build: `make` in this directory (latexmk -pdf). Tables in tables/ are\n% generated by `python -m paper1.latex.make_tables`; the bibliography is\n% checked by `python -m paper1.latex.check_refs`. See README.md here.\n%\n% Class: `article`. The INFORMS Journal on Computing class was not obtained:\n% INFORMS's author pages refused scripted access on 2026-10-03 (HTTP 403), so\n% the template is \"to obtain\" and the formatting below (one column, 11 pt,\n% author-year citations) is a stand-in, not the journal's stated rules.\n\\documentclass[11pt,letterpaper]{article}\n\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[sc]{mathpazo}  % Palatino for text and math (owner, 2026-10-08)\n\\linespread{1.05}         % Palatino needs a little more leading\n\\usepackage{courier}\n\\usepackage[protrusion=true,expansion=false]{microtype}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{colortbl}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{longtable}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\usepackage{float}\n\\floatstyle{ruled}\n\\newfloat{algorithm}{tbp}{loa}[section]\n\\floatname{algorithm}{Algorithm}\n\\usepackage[section]{placeins}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage[hyphens]{url}\n\\usepackage[title,titletoc]{appendix}\n\\usepackage[authoryear,round]{natbib}\n\\usepackage[colorlinks=true,linkcolor=blue!50!black,citecolor=blue!50!black,urlcolor=blue!50!black]{hyperref}\n\n\\graphicspath{{../figures/}}\n\\numberwithin{figure}{section}\n\\numberwithin{table}{section}\n% Yanasse's Pesquisa Operacional paper prints as 1997a and his EJOR paper as\n% 1997b, as in the project's documents: the EJOR key is Yanasse1997b (refs.bib).\n\\captionsetup{font=small,labelfont=bf}\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n% Lean names live in Appendix A (sec9a_appendix_lean.tex), one entry per\n% result. In the text, \\leanref{key} prints \"(Appendix A, A.n)\". In the\n% appendix, \\leanentry{key}{result} opens a row and \\leannames{a, b} prints the\n% names with breaks at the underscores and dots; check_lean.py reads them.\n\\makeatletter\n\\g@addto@macro{\\UrlBreaks}{\\do\\_\\do\\.}\n\\newcommand{\\leannames}[1]{\\def\\lean@sep{}\\@for\\lean@n:=#1\\do{\\lean@sep\\expandafter\\path\\expandafter{\\lean@n}\\def\\lean@sep{, }}}\n\\makeatother\n\\newcounter{leanentry}\n\\renewcommand{\\theleanentry}{A.\\arabic{leanentry}}\n\\newcommand{\\leanentry}[2]{\\leavevmode\\refstepcounter{leanentry}\\label{lean:#1}\\theleanentry & #2 &}\n\\newcommand{\\leangroup}[1]{\\multicolumn{3}{@{}l}{\\emph{#1}}\\\\}\n\\newcommand{\\leanref}[1]{(Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}, \\ref{lean:#1})}\n\n\\theoremstyle{plain}\n\\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}[section]\n\\newtheorem{lemma}[theorem]{Lemma}\n\\newtheorem{proposition}[theorem]{Proposition}\n\\newtheorem{corollary}[theorem]{Corollary}\n\\theoremstyle{definition}\n\\newtheorem{definition}[theorem]{Definition}\n\\newtheorem{counterexample}[theorem]{Counterexample}\n\\theoremstyle{remark}\n\\newtheorem*{remark}{Remark}\n\n\\newcommand{\\pw}{\\mathrm{pw}}\n\\newcommand{\\vs}{\\mathrm{vs}}\n\\newcommand{\\ns}{\\mathrm{ns}}\n\\newcommand{\\mns}{\\mathrm{mns}}\n\\newcommand{\\es}{\\mathrm{es}}\n\\newcommand{\\pes}{\\mathrm{pes}}\n\\newcommand{\\sbw}{\\mathrm{sb}}\n\\newcommand{\\cw}{\\mathrm{cw}}\n\\newcommand{\\mcw}{\\mathrm{mcw}}\n\\newcommand{\\cl}{\\mathrm{cl}}\n\\newcommand{\\Sol}{\\mathrm{Sol}}\n\\newcommand{\\Ext}{\\mathrm{Ext}}\n\\newcommand{\\cost}{\\mathrm{cost}}\n\\newcommand{\\open}{\\mathrm{open}}\n\\newcommand{\\close}{\\mathrm{close}}\n\\newcommand{\\fin}{\\mathrm{fin}}\n\\newcommand{\\pla}{\\mathrm{pla}}\n\\newcommand{\\VSG}{\\mathrm{VSG}}\n% Paths in the paper's repository. They are the paths in this working tree\n% until the repository is built; change them here, in one place, when it is.\n\\newcommand{\\repofile}[1]{\\path{#1}}\n\\newcommand{\\placeholder}[1]{\\par\\noindent\\fbox{\\parbox{\\dimexpr\\linewidth-2\\fboxsep-2\\fboxrule}{\\textbf{Placeholder.} #1}}\\par}\n\n\\title{The pathwidth complex}\n\\author{Alexandre Linhares\\\\{\\small Getulio Vargas Foundation (on leave) and Argo Laboratory}\\\\{\\small \\href{mailto:alex@argolab.org}{alex@argolab.org} \\quad ORCID \\href{https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4227-6879}{0000-0002-4227-6879}} \\and\nHoracio Hideki Yanasse\\\\{\\small Institute of Science and Technology, Federal University of São Paulo (UNIFESP), São José dos Campos, SP, Brazil}\\\\{\\small \\href{mailto:horacio.yanasse@unifesp.br}{horacio.yanasse@unifesp.br} \\quad ORCID \\href{https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6946-9670}{0000-0002-6946-9670}}}\n\\date{19 October 2026}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\n\\noindent\nIn 2001 we listed twelve problems from operations research, VLSI\ndesign and graph theory, the minimization of open stacks problem (MOSP) among\nthem, as equal ``plus or minus one'', and gave no proofs\n\\citep{Linhares2001,LY2002}. We now correct and extend the table, proving formally in Lean that most of\nthem are, mathematically, one quantity, \\emph{graph pathwidth}, studied\nindependently by communities that rarely cite one another. Eight rows are exact,\ncounting pathwidth itself: each equals pathwidth or pathwidth plus one. Two lie\nin \\emph{bands}: they stay within one or two of pathwidth, but not at a fixed\noffset. Two are false as originally stated. We add three members the table missed:\n(i) the maximum wavefront of sparse matrix elimination, which brings a fourth\ndiscipline, numerical linear algebra, into the family; (ii) progressive\npebbling; and (iii) the vertex search of Bienstock, Robertson, Seymour and\nThomas. We call this cluster the\n\\emph{pathwidth complex}, as the literature uses the name \\emph{pathwidth} more\nthan three times as often as the other eleven names combined. Since 2005, the\nnext most active name is MOSP, and the open-stacks community produced, by way\nof that year's Constraint Modelling Challenge, the best exact algorithm for\nMOSP: the \\emph{customer search} of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, which branches on the order in which customers' stacks close (also a\npathwidth algorithm). We prove in Lean that two of its published dominance theorems,\nrules that discard a branch as no better than another, are \\emph{not true},\nand that on a 34-customer counter-example instance their algorithm, with its better move off, reports an\noptimum of 7 where the optimum is 6. Our corrected search (and a SAT solver with a checked DRAT proof) confirms the 6. Our corrected definite move,\nthe first of the two, was also found by Tamaki as a lemma\n\\citep[Lemma~1]{Kitsunai2016} in the pathwidth literature; we prove our\ncorrected search sound, formally, in Lean. We re-prove all 115 benchmark\noptima that rested on Chu and Stuckey's algorithm, changing none. Ported to graphs, our corrected search proves the pathwidth of all 11,534\nRome graphs, a standard graph-drawing benchmark of 10 to 110 vertices (97.1\\% of them within 10 minutes each), and, on every benchmark set with published\nexact counts, as many graphs or more than the published branch and bound of\nCoudert, Mazauric and Nisse (2016). In the other direction, \\emph{from pathwidth to MOSP}, a lower bound from graph\nminors, proved in Lean, settles in seconds instances whose refutation took\ncore-days, and closes the Mycielski graph $M_7$. All of our proofs and solvers are open source.\nWe also release a dataset of 17,714 graphs, distinct up to isomorphism, from\ntwenty benchmark collections for pathwidth and the problems equal to it, the\nMOSP instances and the Rome graphs among them, 16,634 of them with a certified\npathwidth, with independent checkers. We believe ours is currently the\nstrongest exact pathwidth solver openly available.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\setcounter{tocdepth}{2}\n{\\small\\tableofcontents}\n\\clearpage\n\n\\input{sec1_intro}\n\\input{sec2_equivalences}\n\\input{sec3_names}\n\\input{sec4_search}\n\\input{sec5_results}\n\\input{sec6_conclusion}\n\\input{sec7_data}\n\\input{sec8_ack}\n\n\\bibliography{refs}\n\n% Each appendix starts on a new page, headed \"Appendix A\" (appendix package,\n% options title and titletoc: the word in the heading and in the contents).\n\\begin{appendices}\n\\clearpage\\input{sec9a_appendix_lean}\n\\clearpage\\input{sec9b_appendix_machinery}\n\\clearpage\\input{sec9c_appendix_names}\n\\clearpage\\input{sec9d_appendix_sat}\n\\clearpage\\input{sec9e_appendix_random}\n\\end{appendices}\n\n\\end{document}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b64b2670-263a-4912-8a4a-d8fc3e1ebe26","uuid":"20eb57e4-0c5f-4cc3-81d3-b7bd3dfbcd5f","timestamp":"2026-11-01T07:49:51.110Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"% Appendix A: the formal names (loop0009 item 01). Every Lean-name footnote of\n% the draft moved here; the text cites an entry as (Appendix A, A.n) with\n% the leanref macro. check_lean.py and check_axioms.py read the leannames\n% cells. Axiom line printed by paper1/axiom_check.lean (`make axioms`).\n\\section{Formal names}\n\\label{app:lean}\n\nEvery theorem, lemma and counterexample of this paper is checked in the Lean~4\nproof assistant with the Mathlib library, unless the text says otherwise. The\nreader need not read Lean to follow the paper. This appendix is for a reader\nwho wants to find the formal statement of a result: Table~\\ref{tab:lean} maps\neach result, in the order in which it appears, to the names of the Lean\ndeclarations that state it. Where a row cites a definition rather than a\ntheorem, it is the definition the text uses. Lean names use \\texttt{repaired}\nfor what the text calls \\emph{corrected}.\n\nAll the theorems named depend only on the three standard axioms of Lean's\nclassical logic, \\texttt{propext}, \\texttt{Classical.choice} and\n\\texttt{Quot.sound}; an axiom check in the repository prints this for every\ntheorem named in the paper. The development has no \\texttt{sorry}, Lean's marker for a statement left\nunproved.\n\nTwo Lean proofs take a route that the text does not describe. The proof of\nTheorem~\\ref{thm:decides} goes through narrowness \\citep{LY2002ref11}, which\ncounts the closing order forwards rather than reversing it. The proof of\nTheorem~\\ref{thm:matching} reduces the deficiency form of Hall's theorem to\nMathlib's Hall theorem by adding $\\delta$ dummy stacks. One Lean proof takes a\ndifferent route from the published one: the proof\nthat node search is monotone (Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(5)) follows the crusade\nargument of \\citet{BienstockSeymour1991}, not the reduction to edge search of\n\\citet{LaPaugh1993} on which \\citet{LY2002ref10} rely. Three kinds of fact\nare computed rather than proved. The values of $K_2$, $K_{1,3}$ and $K_{3,3}$\nafter Theorem~\\ref{thm:bands}, which show that every end of the two bands\noccurs, are computed by brute force from each quantity's own definition. The\nsearch for smaller counterexamples (Appendix~\\ref{app:minimality}) and the\nresults of Section~\\ref{sec:results} are computations.\n\n{\\small\n\\begin{longtable}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{1.0cm}>{\\raggedright}p{6.6cm}>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{8.0cm}@{}}\n\\caption{Every result of the paper has a formal statement in Lean, listed\nhere in the order in which the results appear.}\\label{tab:lean}\\\\\n\\toprule\nEntry & Result in the paper & Lean names \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\endfirsthead\n\\toprule\nEntry & Result in the paper & Lean names \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\endhead\n\\bottomrule\n\\endfoot\n\\leangroup{Section~\\ref{sec:equiv}, the equivalences}\n\\leanentry{clique}{Lemma~\\ref{lem:clique}, cliques sit in a bag} \\leannames{PathDecomposition.exists_bag_of_isClique} \\\\\n\\leanentry{vs}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:vs}, vertex separation is pathwidth} \\leannames{vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth} \\\\\n\\leanentry{mosp}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp}, MOSP is pathwidth plus one} \\leannames{MOSPInstance.mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{star}{The pattern graph gives no bound: the star $K_{1,4}$ (after Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp})} \\leannames{star_mospValue, star_pathwidth_agreementGraph, star_refutes_pattern_graph_bound} \\\\\n\\leanentry{core-gml}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(1), gate matrix layout} \\leannames{NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_mospValue, NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{core-it}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(2), interval thickness} \\leannames{intervalThickness_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{core-logic}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(3), one-dimensional logic} \\leannames{LogicArray.tracks_eq_intervalThickness, LogicArray.tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{core-narrow}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(4), narrowness} \\leannames{narrowness_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{core-ns}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(5), node search} \\leannames{nodeSearchMonotonicity, nodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one, nodeSearch_chain} \\\\\n\\leanentry{core-fold}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(6), multiple folding} \\leannames{NetGateMatrix.foldTracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{core-vsg}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}(7), Lengauer's vertex separator game} \\leannames{vsg_eq_max} \\\\\n\\leanentry{bands}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:bands}, the bands} \\leannames{pathwidth_le_splitBandwidth_le_pathwidth_add_one, vertexSeparation_le_edgeSearch_le_add_two, vertexSeparation_le_progressiveEdgeSearch_le_add_two} \\\\\n\\leanentry{false-pla}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:false}(1), PLA folding} \\leannames{plaTracks_idMatrix_five, plaTracks_idMatrix_unbounded, plaTracks_pathMatrix_unbounded} \\\\\n\\leanentry{false-cw}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:false}(2), edge separation read as cutwidth} \\leannames{cutwidth_unbounded, modCutwidth_unbounded} \\\\\n\\leangroup{Section~\\ref{sec:search}, the search}\n\\leanentry{defs}{Step cost, $\\Ext_k$ and $\\Sol_k$ (Section~\\ref{sec:search-def})} \\leannames{stepCost, Solvable, SearchSol} \\\\\n\\leanentry{F}{Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}, free moves never hurt} \\leannames{solvable_iff_searchSol_cl} \\\\\n\\leanentry{decides}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:decides}, the search decides pathwidth} \\leannames{searchSol_empty_iff_pathwidth_add_one_le, searchSol_mospGraph_iff_mospValue_le} \\\\\n\\leanentry{dict1}{Table~\\ref{tab:dictionary}: closing order from $\\emptyset$} \\leannames{orderOfLayout} \\\\\n\\leanentry{dict2}{Table~\\ref{tab:dictionary}: open stacks $b(T)$} \\leannames{openStacks_eq_card_boundary} \\\\\n\\leanentry{dict3}{Table~\\ref{tab:dictionary}: $\\cost(T,c)$} \\leannames{stepCost_eq_card_boundary_add_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{dict4}{Table~\\ref{tab:dictionary}: $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$} \\leannames{searchSol_empty_iff_pathwidth_add_one_le} \\\\\n\\leanentry{dict5}{Table~\\ref{tab:dictionary}: free closure $\\cl(T)$} \\leannames{mem_cl_iff} \\\\\n\\leanentry{dict6}{Table~\\ref{tab:dictionary}: definite move, corrected} \\leannames{isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_isCommittable} \\\\\n\\leanentry{premise}{Lemma~\\ref{lem:premise}, what the premise says} \\leannames{closeCount_eq, isDefinite_iff} \\\\\n\\leanentry{cex}{Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite}} \\leannames{definiteMove_counterexample, cexFamily_closed} \\\\\n\\leanentry{thm1false}{Chu and Stuckey's Theorem~1 (the definite move), under both readings, and Chu's Theorem~6.3.6 are false} \\leannames{chuStuckey_theorem1_false, chuStuckey_theorem1_false_literal, chuThesis_theorem636_false} \\\\\n\\leanentry{fink}{Fink's restatement of the definite move is false} \\leannames{fink_theorem1_false} \\\\\n\\leanentry{hered}{The hereditarily definite premise, and its failure in Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite}} \\leannames{IsHereditarilyDefinite, not_isHereditarilyDefinite_cex} \\\\\n\\leanentry{submod}{Lemma~\\ref{lem:submod}, open stacks are submodular} \\leannames{openStacks_submodular} \\\\\n\\leanentry{repaired}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:repaired}, the corrected definite move is sound} \\leannames{solvable_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite, searchSol_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite} \\\\\n\\leanentry{matching}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:matching}, the corrected premise is a matching condition} \\leannames{isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_hasDefiniteMatching} \\\\\n\\leanentry{tamaki}{Lemma~1 of \\citet{Kitsunai2016}, transcribed for digraphs in their terms and proved by their argument; on the symmetric digraph with the extension fixed to the child, its commitment condition is the hereditary premise, our theorem follows from it under its two feasibility hypotheses, it follows from ours, and a five-vertex graph shows those hypotheses are not redundant} \\leannames{Tamaki.lemma1, Tamaki.inDeg_submodular, Tamaki.isCommittableExt_symm_iff_hereditarilyDefinite, Tamaki.solvable_cl_insert_of_lemma1, Tamaki.lemma1_symm_of_isCommittable, Tamaki.premise_gap} \\\\\n\\leanentry{open1}{The published definite move is right when $\\open(q,S)\\le1$} \\leannames{isHereditarilyDefinite_of_openCount_le_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{subset}{The subset rule is sound} \\leannames{searchSol_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset, subsetFilter_sound} \\\\\n\\leanentry{tiebreak}{The subset rule needs its tie-break (7-customer graph)} \\leannames{noTieBreak_counterexample} \\\\\n\\leanentry{isbetter}{The better move's premise, restated} \\leannames{isBetter_iff} \\\\\n\\leanentry{bettercex}{Chu and Stuckey's Theorem~2 (the better move) is false} \\leannames{betterMove_counterexample, chuStuckey_theorem2_false} \\\\\n\\leanentry{thm638}{Chu's Theorem~6.3.8 is false} \\leannames{chuThesis_theorem638_false, chuThesis_theorem638_false_literal} \\\\\n\\leanentry{repbetter}{The corrected better move is sound} \\leannames{IsRepairedBetter, searchSol_cl_insert_of_repairedBetter} \\\\\n\\leanentry{bugs}{The better move's two requirements: the close count and the rule order} \\leannames{bugA_counterexample, bugB_counterexample} \\\\\n\\leanentry{oldmove}{The old move is sound} \\leannames{searchSol_reinsert, not_searchSol_of_oldMove} \\\\\n\\leanentry{reinsert}{The old move needs its reinsertion test (path on five vertices)} \\leannames{reinsert_needs_test} \\\\\n\\leanentry{memo}{Solvability of a state does not depend on the path to it (the memo)} \\leannames{solvable_iff_of_cl_eq} \\\\\n\\leanentry{fakeold}{A node with an unrefuted old move can record a solvable state} \\leannames{exec_fake_oldMove} \\\\\n\\leanentry{nodesound}{Node-soundness of a filter (Section~\\ref{sec:theorem})} \\leannames{NodeSoundAt} \\\\\n\\leanentry{main}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:main}, our corrected search is sound} \\leannames{exec_repairedFullFilter_sound, exec_repairedFullFilter_pathwidth, exec_repairedFullFilter_mospValue} \\\\\n\\leanentry{notsound}{Under the previous algorithm the filter is not node-sound} \\leannames{not_codeFilterSound_cexGraph} \\\\\n\\leanentry{wrong}{Figure~\\ref{fig:wrong}: the previous algorithm refutes 6 and finds 7 on the 34-customer instance, whose optimum is 6 and pathwidth 5; its run is a run of the search with the published filter, which is therefore not sound there} \\leannames{previousAlgorithm_wrong_optimum, not_filterSound_codeFilter} \\\\\n\\leanentry{minor}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:minor}: contracting an edge (merging $v$ into $u$, as the solvers do) or deleting a vertex or an edge never increases pathwidth; along a legal contraction sequence, a minor of pathwidth above $w$ forces pathwidth above $w$, and on the MOSP graph more than $w+1$ open stacks} \\leannames{pathwidth_contractEdge_le, pathwidth_induce_le, pathwidth_mono, pathwidth_minorOf_le, pathwidth_gt_of_minorOf, MOSPInstance.lt_mospValue_of_minorOf} \\\\\n\\leanentry{grid}{The $(h,w)$-grid has pathwidth $\\min(h,w)$, except the $1\\times1$ grid (pathwidth $0$); \\citet[Theorem~4.1]{EllisWarren2008}. Upper bound by the row-major layout's bandwidth; lower bound directly on vertex separation, at the first prefix that completes a row or a column} \\leannames{pathwidth_grid, pathwidth_grid_le, min_le_pathwidth_grid, pathwidth_grid_one_one, pathwidth_grid_square} \\\\\n\\leanentry{hypercube}{The $d$-dimensional hypercube has pathwidth $\\sum_{i<d}\\binom{i}{\\lfloor i/2\\rfloor}$ \\citep[Theorem~2]{ChandranKavitha2006}, with Harper's vertex-isoperimetric theorem \\citep{Harper1966} proved by compression. Upper bound by the layout along Harper's simplicial order, lower bound at a prefix of every size, both by induction on $d$ and on vertex separation} \\leannames{pathwidth_hypercube, pathwidth_cubeGraph, Hypercube.harper, Hypercube.card_nbhd_init_le, Hypercube.exists_init_profile} \\\\\n\\leanentry{mycielski}{For every graph $G$, its Mycielskian $\\mu(G)$ (originals, a shadow of each vertex adjacent to its neighbours, and an apex adjacent to the shadows) has $\\mathrm{pw}(G)+1\\le\\mathrm{pw}(\\mu(G))\\le 2\\,\\mathrm{pw}(G)+1$. The upper bound interleaves each vertex with its shadow and places the apex last; we found no published statement of it. The lower bound keeps the bags meeting the apex and the shadows} \\leannames{pathwidth_mycielskian_le, pathwidth_add_one_le_mycielskian} \\\\\n\\leanentry{emptypair}{Step~1 of Appendix~\\ref{app:random}: at every position of every layout the prefix and the inactive suffix are an empty pair, so for every $j\\le n$ some $j$-set has closed neighbourhood of size at most $j+\\pw$ (Harper's bound in layout form), and $n-2s^{*}-2\\le\\pw$ for the empty-pair index $s^{*}$} \\leannames{exists_empty_pair_of_layout, exists_closedNbhd_card_le, exists_empty_pair, card_sub_two_mul_emptyPairIndex_sub_two_le_pathwidth} \\\\\n\\leanentry{firstmoment}{Step~2 of Appendix~\\ref{app:random}: the number of graphs on $n$ labelled vertices of pathwidth at most $k$ is at most $\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}2^{\\binom n2-t(t-1)}$, $t=\\lceil(n-k)/2\\rceil$, and the $G(n,p)$-weighted sum of those graphs is at most $\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}(1-p)^{t(t-1)}$; there are $2^{\\binom n2}$ graphs on $n$ labelled vertices} \\leannames{card_pathwidth_le_le, weightedSum_graphsOfPathwidthLe_le, card_emptyPairGraphs, card_simpleGraph} \\\\\n\\leanentry{firstmomentA}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment} and its consequence: the weighted sum is at most $\\exp(-\\delta n+3\\ln n+ca)$ for $n\\ge\\max(3,c)$, at most $e^{c/2}n^{3}e^{-\\delta n}$, and tends to $0$ when $\\delta>0$; the trinomial entropy bound from the binomial theorem} \\leannames{firstMoment_pathwidth_le_fin, firstMoment_pathwidth_le_fin', tendsto_firstMoment_pathwidth, choose_mul_choose_le_exp_entropy} \\\\\n\\leanentry{clique-cover}{Appendix~\\ref{app:random-cs}: for independent uniform patterns of given sizes, the probability that the instance's MOSP value is at most $k+1$ (its pathwidth at most $k$) is at most $\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}\\prod_j N(s_j)/\\binom n{s_j}$, $N(s)$ the number of $s$-sets missing $A$ or missing $R$} \\leannames{prob_coversOfMospValueLe_le, prob_coversOfPathwidthLe_le, card_coversOfPathwidthLe_le, card_filter_avoid} \\\\\n\\leanentry{endpoint}{Lemma~\\ref{lem:endpoint}, the premises as commitment conditions} \\leannames{isDefinite_iff_endpoint, isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_isCommittable} \\\\\n\\leanentry{cexnotcommit}{In Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} the endpoint test passes and the commitment fails} \\leannames{cex_isDefinite_not_isCommittable} \\\\\n\\leanentry{existscommit}{The published premise gives a commitment to the least-border set} \\leannames{exists_isCommittable_of_isDefinite} \\\\\n\\leanentry{commit234}{In Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} that set is $\\{2,3,4\\}$} \\leannames{cex_isCommittable_234} \\\\\n\\leanentry{depth1}{A commitment of depth one holds iff $\\open(q,S)\\le1$} \\leannames{isCommittable_insert_iff} \\\\\n\\leanentry{greedy}{The greedy step of Coudert, Mazauric and Nisse is that condition} \\leannames{isGreedyStep_iff} \\\\\n\\leangroup{Section~\\ref{sec:results}, computational results}\n\\leanentry{split}{The split of the root into independent tasks is sound} \\leannames{execSplit_repairedFullFilter_sound, execSplit_repairedFullFilter_mospValue} \\\\\n\\leangroup{Appendix~\\ref{app:machinery-all}, supporting material}\n\\leanentry{edgeless-ns}{Node search equalities need an edge (Appendix~\\ref{app:edge})} \\leannames{nodeSearch_ne_intervalThickness_of_edgeless, monotoneNodeSearch_ne_vertexSeparation_add_one_of_edgeless} \\\\\n\\leanentry{vsg0}{Lengauer's Theorem~4 fails at $k=0$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:edge})} \\leannames{isPositiveVSG_triangleGraph_counterexample} \\\\\n\\leanentry{tns}{$t=\\ns$ fails on the matrix $[1]$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:edge})} \\leannames{tracks_ne_monotoneNodeSearch_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{kp}{The Kirousis--Papadimitriou claim fails on $K_{1,3}$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:edge})} \\leannames{kirousisPapadimitriou_claim2_false} \\\\\n\\leanentry{pebbling}{Progressive pebbling is in the exact core (Appendix~\\ref{app:thirteenth})} \\leannames{pbw_lengauerD_eq_pathwidth, pbw_eq_mospValue_pebbleMatrix, mpb_eq_pathwidth_add_one} \\\\\n\\leanentry{vsearch}{The vertex search is in the exact core (Appendix~\\ref{app:thirteenth})} \\leannames{vertexSearchNumber_eq_pathwidth_add_one, monotoneVertexSearchNumber_eq_pathwidth_add_one, isNodeSearch_of_isSuccessfulVertexSearch, vertexSearchNumber_eq_nodeSearch, vertexSearchNumber_of_isEmpty} \\\\\n\\leanentry{wavefront}{The maximum wavefront is in the exact core (Appendix~\\ref{app:thirteenth})} \\leannames{card_wavefront, maxWavefront_eq_vertexSepOfLayout_add_one, minMaxWavefront_eq_pathwidth_add_one, minMaxWavefront_eq_narrowness, minMaxWavefront_of_isEmpty} \\\\\n\\leanentry{filter}{Proposition~\\ref{prop:filter}, the corrected filter is node-sound} \\leannames{repairedFullFilter_sound} \\\\\n\\leanentry{runs}{Theorem~\\ref{thm:runs}, runs are sound} \\leannames{Exec.sound} \\\\\n\\leanentry{encoding}{Appendix~\\ref{app:sat}, the SAT formulation is faithful (clauses 1--4)} \\leannames{encodes_iff_mospValue_le} \\\\\n\\end{longtable}\n}\n----\n% The pathwidth complex -- first full draft (Ralph loop0008 item 08, 2026-10-03).\n%\n% Build: `make` in this directory (latexmk -pdf). Tables in tables/ are\n% generated by `python -m paper1.latex.make_tables`; the bibliography is\n% checked by `python -m paper1.latex.check_refs`. See README.md here.\n%\n% Class: `article`. The INFORMS Journal on Computing class was not obtained:\n% INFORMS's author pages refused scripted access on 2026-10-03 (HTTP 403), so\n% the template is \"to obtain\" and the formatting below (one column, 11 pt,\n% author-year citations) is a stand-in, not the journal's stated rules.\n\\documentclass[11pt,letterpaper]{article}\n\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[sc]{mathpazo}  % Palatino for text and math (owner, 2026-10-08)\n\\linespread{1.05}         % Palatino needs a little more leading\n\\usepackage{courier}\n\\usepackage[protrusion=true,expansion=false]{microtype}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{colortbl}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{longtable}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\usepackage{float}\n\\floatstyle{ruled}\n\\newfloat{algorithm}{tbp}{loa}[section]\n\\floatname{algorithm}{Algorithm}\n\\usepackage[section]{placeins}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage[hyphens]{url}\n\\usepackage[title,titletoc]{appendix}\n\\usepackage[authoryear,round]{natbib}\n\\usepackage[colorlinks=true,linkcolor=blue!50!black,citecolor=blue!50!black,urlcolor=blue!50!black]{hyperref}\n\n\\graphicspath{{../figures/}}\n\\numberwithin{figure}{section}\n\\numberwithin{table}{section}\n% Yanasse's Pesquisa Operacional paper prints as 1997a and his EJOR paper as\n% 1997b, as in the project's documents: the EJOR key is Yanasse1997b (refs.bib).\n\\captionsetup{font=small,labelfont=bf}\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n% Lean names live in Appendix A (sec9a_appendix_lean.tex), one entry per\n% result. In the text, \\leanref{key} prints \"(Appendix A, A.n)\". In the\n% appendix, \\leanentry{key}{result} opens a row and \\leannames{a, b} prints the\n% names with breaks at the underscores and dots; check_lean.py reads them.\n\\makeatletter\n\\g@addto@macro{\\UrlBreaks}{\\do\\_\\do\\.}\n\\newcommand{\\leannames}[1]{\\def\\lean@sep{}\\@for\\lean@n:=#1\\do{\\lean@sep\\expandafter\\path\\expandafter{\\lean@n}\\def\\lean@sep{, }}}\n\\makeatother\n\\newcounter{leanentry}\n\\renewcommand{\\theleanentry}{A.\\arabic{leanentry}}\n\\newcommand{\\leanentry}[2]{\\leavevmode\\refstepcounter{leanentry}\\label{lean:#1}\\theleanentry & #2 &}\n\\newcommand{\\leangroup}[1]{\\multicolumn{3}{@{}l}{\\emph{#1}}\\\\}\n\\newcommand{\\leanref}[1]{(Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}, \\ref{lean:#1})}\n\n\\theoremstyle{plain}\n\\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}[section]\n\\newtheorem{lemma}[theorem]{Lemma}\n\\newtheorem{proposition}[theorem]{Proposition}\n\\newtheorem{corollary}[theorem]{Corollary}\n\\theoremstyle{definition}\n\\newtheorem{definition}[theorem]{Definition}\n\\newtheorem{counterexample}[theorem]{Counterexample}\n\\theoremstyle{remark}\n\\newtheorem*{remark}{Remark}\n\n\\newcommand{\\pw}{\\mathrm{pw}}\n\\newcommand{\\vs}{\\mathrm{vs}}\n\\newcommand{\\ns}{\\mathrm{ns}}\n\\newcommand{\\mns}{\\mathrm{mns}}\n\\newcommand{\\es}{\\mathrm{es}}\n\\newcommand{\\pes}{\\mathrm{pes}}\n\\newcommand{\\sbw}{\\mathrm{sb}}\n\\newcommand{\\cw}{\\mathrm{cw}}\n\\newcommand{\\mcw}{\\mathrm{mcw}}\n\\newcommand{\\cl}{\\mathrm{cl}}\n\\newcommand{\\Sol}{\\mathrm{Sol}}\n\\newcommand{\\Ext}{\\mathrm{Ext}}\n\\newcommand{\\cost}{\\mathrm{cost}}\n\\newcommand{\\open}{\\mathrm{open}}\n\\newcommand{\\close}{\\mathrm{close}}\n\\newcommand{\\fin}{\\mathrm{fin}}\n\\newcommand{\\pla}{\\mathrm{pla}}\n\\newcommand{\\VSG}{\\mathrm{VSG}}\n% Paths in the paper's repository. They are the paths in this working tree\n% until the repository is built; change them here, in one place, when it is.\n\\newcommand{\\repofile}[1]{\\path{#1}}\n\\newcommand{\\placeholder}[1]{\\par\\noindent\\fbox{\\parbox{\\dimexpr\\linewidth-2\\fboxsep-2\\fboxrule}{\\textbf{Placeholder.} #1}}\\par}\n\n\\title{The pathwidth complex}\n\\author{Alexandre Linhares\\\\{\\small Getulio Vargas Foundation (on leave) and Argo Laboratory}\\\\{\\small \\href{mailto:alex@argolab.org}{alex@argolab.org} \\quad ORCID \\href{https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4227-6879}{0000-0002-4227-6879}} \\and\nHoracio Hideki Yanasse\\\\{\\small Institute of Science and Technology, Federal University of São Paulo (UNIFESP), São José dos Campos, SP, Brazil}\\\\{\\small \\href{mailto:horacio.yanasse@unifesp.br}{horacio.yanasse@unifesp.br} \\quad ORCID \\href{https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6946-9670}{0000-0002-6946-9670}}}\n\\date{19 October 2026}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\n\\noindent\nIn 2001 we listed twelve problems from operations research, VLSI\ndesign and graph theory, the minimization of open stacks problem (MOSP) among\nthem, as equal ``plus or minus one'', and gave no proofs\n\\citep{Linhares2001,LY2002}. We now correct and extend the table, proving formally in Lean that most of\nthem are, mathematically, one quantity, \\emph{graph pathwidth}, studied\nindependently by communities that rarely cite one another. Eight rows are exact,\ncounting pathwidth itself: each equals pathwidth or pathwidth plus one. Two lie\nin \\emph{bands}: they stay within one or two of pathwidth, but not at a fixed\noffset. Two are false as originally stated. We add three members the table missed:\n(i) the maximum wavefront of sparse matrix elimination, which brings a fourth\ndiscipline, numerical linear algebra, into the family; (ii) progressive\npebbling; and (iii) the vertex search of Bienstock, Robertson, Seymour and\nThomas. We call this cluster the\n\\emph{pathwidth complex}, as the literature uses the name \\emph{pathwidth} more\nthan three times as often as the other eleven names combined. Since 2005, the\nnext most active name is MOSP, and the open-stacks community produced, by way\nof that year's Constraint Modelling Challenge, the best exact algorithm for\nMOSP: the \\emph{customer search} of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, which branches on the order in which customers' stacks close (also a\npathwidth algorithm). We prove in Lean that two of its published dominance theorems,\nrules that discard a branch as no better than another, are \\emph{not true},\nand that on a 34-customer counter-example instance their algorithm, with its better move off, reports an\noptimum of 7 where the optimum is 6. Our corrected search (and a SAT solver with a checked DRAT proof) confirms the 6. Our corrected definite move,\nthe first of the two, was also found by Tamaki as a lemma\n\\citep[Lemma~1]{Kitsunai2016} in the pathwidth literature; we prove our\ncorrected search sound, formally, in Lean. We re-prove all 115 benchmark\noptima that rested on Chu and Stuckey's algorithm, changing none. Ported to graphs, our corrected search proves the pathwidth of all 11,534\nRome graphs, a standard graph-drawing benchmark of 10 to 110 vertices (97.1\\% of them within 10 minutes each), and, on every benchmark set with published\nexact counts, as many graphs or more than the published branch and bound of\nCoudert, Mazauric and Nisse (2016). In the other direction, \\emph{from pathwidth to MOSP}, a lower bound from graph\nminors, proved in Lean, settles in seconds instances whose refutation took\ncore-days, and closes the Mycielski graph $M_7$. All of our proofs and solvers are open source.\nWe also release a dataset of 17,714 graphs, distinct up to isomorphism, from\ntwenty benchmark collections for pathwidth and the problems equal to it, the\nMOSP instances and the Rome graphs among them, 16,634 of them with a certified\npathwidth, with independent checkers. We believe ours is currently the\nstrongest exact pathwidth solver openly available.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\setcounter{tocdepth}{2}\n{\\small\\tableofcontents}\n\\clearpage\n\n\\input{sec1_intro}\n\\input{sec2_equivalences}\n\\input{sec3_names}\n\\input{sec4_search}\n\\input{sec5_results}\n\\input{sec6_conclusion}\n\\input{sec7_data}\n\\input{sec8_ack}\n\n\\bibliography{refs}\n\n% Each appendix starts on a new page, headed \"Appendix A\" (appendix package,\n% options title and titletoc: the word in the heading and in the contents).\n\\begin{appendices}\n\\clearpage\\input{sec9a_appendix_lean}\n\\clearpage\\input{sec9b_appendix_machinery}\n\\clearpage\\input{sec9c_appendix_names}\n\\clearpage\\input{sec9d_appendix_sat}\n\\clearpage\\input{sec9e_appendix_random}\n\\end{appendices}\n\n\\end{document}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018LKwKBCzH2hztHwQQjQDdQ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011Cgbqn3W8ip2mySrRYA7Y5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RCSVcJCaCPf1j6jGiQkJEW","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat paper1/latex/Makefile && echo ---- && cat paper1/latex/check_refs.py && echo ---- && cat paper1/latex/check_axioms.py && echo ---- && ls paper1/latex/data paper1/latex/tables && cat paper1/latex/README.md","description":"Read the Makefile, ref and axiom checkers, README"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3304,"cache_read_input_tokens":98612,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3304,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b64b2670-263a-4912-8a4a-d8fc3e1ebe26","uuid":"1c01e065-b0f8-434c-a747-ae1de6e9f7e0","timestamp":"2026-11-01T07:49:52.740Z","request_id":"req_011Cgbqn374zpdn5yx9yUDPJ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01RCSVcJCaCPf1j6jGiQkJEW":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP && cat paper1/latex/Makefile && echo ---- && cat paper1/latex/check_refs.py && echo ---- && cat paper1/latex/check_axioms.py && echo ---- && ls paper1/latex/data paper1/latex/tables && cat paper1/latex/README.md","description":"Read the Makefile, ref and axiom checkers, README"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01RCSVcJCaCPf1j6jGiQkJEW":{"cwd":"/home/al/dev/MOSP"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RCSVcJCaCPf1j6jGiQkJEW","type":"tool_result","content":"# Builds paper1/latex/main.pdf, the first full draft of paper 2.\n#   make          latexmk -pdf (pdflatex + bibtex as often as needed)\n#   make tables   regenerate tables/*.tex from the recorded CSVs\n#   make refs     check every DOI in refs.bib against Crossref / DataCite (cached)\n#   make axioms   every theorem the draft cites has a clean axiom line (needs lake build)\n#   make check    fail on undefined references or citations in the last build\n#   make clean    remove build products but the PDF\nall: main.pdf\n\nmain.pdf: main.tex $(wildcard sec*.tex) refs.bib $(wildcard tables/*.tex) $(wildcard ../figures/*.pdf)\n\tlatexmk -pdf -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error main.tex\n\ntables:\n\tcd ../.. && python3 -m paper1.latex.make_tables\n\nrefs:\n\tcd ../.. && python3 -m paper1.latex.check_refs\n\naxioms:\n\tcd ../.. && python3 -m paper1.latex.check_axioms\n\ncheck: main.pdf\n\t@! grep -E \"undefined|Citation .* undefined|Reference .* undefined\" main.log\n\t@echo \"no undefined references; $$(pdfinfo main.pdf | grep Pages)\"\n\nclean:\n\tlatexmk -c\n\trm -f *.bbl *.blg *.synctex.gz\n\ndistclean: clean\n\trm -f main.pdf\n\n.PHONY: all tables refs axioms check clean distclean\n----\n\"\"\"Check every DOI in paper1/latex/refs.bib against its registry (loop0008 item 08).\n\n    python -m paper1.latex.check_refs            # uses the cache, fetches what is missing\n    python -m paper1.latex.check_refs --refresh  # fetches everything again\n\nFor each entry with a `doi`, fetch the record from Crossref, or from DataCite\nwhen Crossref does not know it (LIPIcs, arXiv), and compare the title, the\nyear and the first author's family name with the entry. The responses are\ncached in `paper1/latex/data/doi_check.json` with the date of the fetch.\nPrints one line per entry and exits non-zero if a title or first author\ndisagrees. A year that differs is reported, not failed: Crossref often gives\nthe online-first year where the bibliography gives the volume's.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport json\nimport re\nimport sys\nimport unicodedata\nimport urllib.request\nfrom datetime import date\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\nBIB = HERE / \"refs.bib\"\nCACHE = HERE / \"data\" / \"doi_check.json\"\n# Disagreements read by hand on 2026-10-03 and accepted, with the reason.\nKNOWN = {\n    \"LY2002ref8\": \"Crossref puts the whole name, 'Omar Wing', in the family field\",\n    \"LY2002ref9\": \"Crossref carries the publisher's typo 'Interval graphs and seatching'\",\n    \"Kitsunai2016\": \"Crossref's title carries MathML residue; the words agree\",\n    \"ChandranKavitha2006\": \"Crossref puts 'Sunil Chandran' in the family field; the author is L. Sunil Chandran\",\n}\nUA = {\"User-Agent\": \"paper1-refcheck/0.1 (mailto:none@example.org)\"}\n\n\ndef entries(text: str) -> list[dict]:\n    out = []\n    for m in re.finditer(r\"@(\\w+)\\{([^,]+),(.*?)\\n\\}\", text, re.S):\n        body = m.group(3)\n        fields = {k.lower(): re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \" \", v).strip()\n                  for k, v in re.findall(r\"(\\w+)\\s*=\\s*\\{((?:[^{}]|\\{(?:[^{}]|\\{[^{}]*\\})*\\})*)\\}\", body)}\n        fields[\"key\"], fields[\"type\"] = m.group(2).strip(), m.group(1).lower()\n        out.append(fields)\n    return out\n\n\ndef norm(s: str) -> str:\n    s = re.sub(r\"\\\\[a-zA-Z]+\\s*|[{}$\\\\^]\", \"\", s)\n    s = unicodedata.normalize(\"NFKD\", s).encode(\"ascii\", \"ignore\").decode()\n    return re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]\", \"\", s.lower())\n\n\ndef fetch(doi: str) -> dict:\n    try:\n        req = urllib.request.Request(f\"https://api.crossref.org/works/{doi}\", headers=UA)\n        m = json.load(urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=30))[\"message\"]\n        year = (m.get(\"issued\", {}).get(\"date-parts\") or [[None]])[0][0]\n        return {\"registry\": \"crossref\", \"title\": (m.get(\"title\") or [\"\"])[0], \"year\": year,\n                \"first_author\": (m.get(\"author\") or [{}])[0].get(\"family\", \"\"),\n                \"fetched\": date.today().isoformat()}\n    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - fall through to DataCite\n        req = urllib.request.Request(f\"https://api.datacite.org/dois/{doi}\", headers=UA)\n        a = json.load(urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=30))[\"data\"][\"attributes\"]\n        return {\"registry\": \"datacite\", \"title\": a[\"titles\"][0][\"title\"],\n                \"year\": int(a[\"publicationYear\"]),\n                \"first_author\": a[\"creators\"][0].get(\"familyName\", \"\"),\n                \"fetched\": date.today().isoformat()}\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.split(\"\\n\")[0])\n    ap.add_argument(\"--refresh\", action=\"store_true\")\n    args = ap.parse_args()\n    cache = {} if args.refresh or not CACHE.exists() else json.loads(CACHE.read_text())\n    bad = 0\n    es = entries(BIB.read_text())\n    for e in es:\n        doi = e.get(\"doi\")\n        if not doi:\n            print(f\"--   {e['key']:28s} no DOI (checked by hand, see its comment)\")\n            continue\n        if doi.lower() not in cache:\n            cache[doi.lower()] = fetch(doi)\n        r = cache[doi.lower()]\n        t_ok = norm(r[\"title\"]).startswith(norm(e[\"title\"])[:40]) or \\\n            norm(e[\"title\"]).startswith(norm(r[\"title\"])[:40])\n        first = e.get(\"author\", \"\").split(\" and \")[0].split(\",\")[0]\n        a_ok = norm(r[\"first_author\"]) == norm(first) or norm(first).endswith(norm(r[\"first_author\"]))\n        y_ok = str(r[\"year\"]) == e.get(\"year\")\n        status = \"ok\" if t_ok and a_ok else (\"known\" if e[\"key\"] in KNOWN else \"BAD\")\n        bad += status == \"BAD\"\n        note = \"\" if y_ok else f\" (year: bib {e.get('year')}, {r['registry']} {r['year']})\"\n        print(f\"{status:5s} {e['key']:28s} {r['registry']:8s} {doi}{note}\")\n        if status != \"ok\":\n            print(f\"     registry: {r['title']!r} / {r['first_author']}\"\n                  + (f\"; accepted: {KNOWN[e['key']]}\" if status == \"known\" else \"\"))\n    CACHE.parent.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    CACHE.write_text(json.dumps(cache, indent=1, sort_keys=True) + \"\\n\")\n    print(f\"{len(es)} entries, {sum(1 for e in es if e.get('doi'))} with a DOI, {bad} disagree\")\n    return 1 if bad else 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n----\n\"\"\"Axiom audit of every theorem the draft cites (loop0008 item 09).\n\n    python -m paper1.latex.check_axioms            # coverage, then run Lean\n    python -m paper1.latex.check_axioms --no-lean  # coverage only (no build)\n\nTwo checks.\n\n1. **Coverage.** Every theorem or lemma named in a `\\\\lean{...}` footnote or a\n   `\\\\path{...}` cell of `sec*.tex` has a `#print axioms` line in\n   `paper1/axiom_check.lean`. Definitions (`def`, `structure`, ...) are\n   skipped: they carry no proof.\n2. **Axioms.** `lake env lean ../paper1/axiom_check.lean` is run from `lean/`,\n   and every line must report exactly `propext`, `Classical.choice` and\n   `Quot.sound` (until 2026-10-31 `conjecture_sqrt_tw_f6` was a control\n   expected to show `sorryAx`; it is proved now). Needs a built `lean/`\n   (`lake build`), takes a few seconds.\n\nExits non-zero on any failure.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport re\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nfrom paper1.latex.check_lean import LEAN, cited\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]\nAXIOM_CHECK = ROOT / \"paper1\" / \"axiom_check.lean\"\nEXPECTED = {\"propext\", \"Classical.choice\", \"Quot.sound\"}\nTHEOREM = re.compile(r\"^\\s*(?:@\\[[^\\]]*\\]\\s*)?(?:private\\s+|protected\\s+)*\"\n                     r\"(?:theorem|lemma)\\s+([^\\s:({\\[]+)\", re.M)\n\n\ndef theorem_names() -> set[str]:\n    \"\"\"Last components of every theorem or lemma under lean/MOSPFormalization/.\"\"\"\n    names: set[str] = set()\n    for p in LEAN.rglob(\"*.lean\"):\n        names.update(m.group(1).split(\".\")[-1] for m in THEOREM.finditer(p.read_text()))\n    return names\n\n\ndef printed() -> set[str]:\n    \"\"\"Last components of the names `axiom_check.lean` prints.\"\"\"\n    return {line.split()[-1].split(\".\")[-1]\n            for line in AXIOM_CHECK.read_text().splitlines()\n            if line.startswith(\"#print axioms\")}\n\n\ndef uncovered() -> list[str]:\n    theorems, have = theorem_names(), printed()\n    return sorted(n for n in cited()\n                  if n.split(\".\")[-1] in theorems and n.split(\".\")[-1] not in have)\n\n\ndef run_lean() -> tuple[int, list[str]]:\n    \"\"\"Run the axiom check; return (lines checked, failures).\"\"\"\n    out = subprocess.run([\"lake\", \"env\", \"lean\", str(AXIOM_CHECK)], cwd=ROOT / \"lean\",\n                         capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)\n    text = out.stdout + out.stderr\n    failures = [] if out.returncode == 0 else [f\"lean exited {out.returncode}\"]\n    records = re.findall(r\"'(\\S+)' depends on axioms: \\[([^\\]]*)\\]\", text)\n    records += [(n, \"\") for n in re.findall(r\"'(\\S+)' does not depend on any axioms\", text)]\n    for name, axioms in records:\n        found = {a.strip() for a in axioms.split(\",\") if a.strip()}\n        if found != EXPECTED:\n            failures.append(f\"{name}: {sorted(found)}\")\n    expected = sum(line.startswith(\"#print axioms\")\n                   for line in AXIOM_CHECK.read_text().splitlines())\n    if len(records) != expected:\n        failures.append(f\"{len(records)} axiom lines for {expected} #print lines\")\n    return len(records), failures\n\n\ndef main(argv: list[str]) -> int:\n    missing = uncovered()\n    for n in missing:\n        print(f\"NOT IN axiom_check.lean: {n}\")\n    print(f\"{len(cited())} names cited, {len(missing)} theorems without an axiom line\")\n    if missing:\n        return 1\n    if \"--no-lean\" in argv:\n        return 0\n    n, failures = run_lean()\n    for f in failures:\n        print(f\"FAIL {f}\")\n    print(f\"{n} theorems checked, {len(failures)} failures\")\n    return 1 if failures else 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))\n----\npaper1/latex/data:\ndoi_check.json\n\npaper1/latex/tables:\ncertificates.tex\ncommunities.tex\ncost.tex\ncs_firstmoment.tex\ndataset.tex\ngraphs.tex\nnames.tex\nperiods.tex\nsplit.tex\n# *The pathwidth complex*: the LaTeX draft\n\nFirst full draft, Ralph loop0008 item 08, 2026-10-03; item 09 added Figure 3.1 and\nthe axiom audit. **25 pages** (article\nclass, 11 pt, one column, US letter, 1 in margins), no undefined references or\ncitations, no overfull boxes.\n\n```bash\ncd paper1/latex\nmake            # latexmk -pdf -> main.pdf\nmake check      # fails on any undefined reference; prints the page count\nmake tables     # regenerate tables/*.tex from the recorded CSVs (python -m paper1.latex.make_tables)\nmake refs       # check every DOI of refs.bib against Crossref / DataCite (python -m paper1.latex.check_refs)\nmake axioms     # every theorem the draft cites has a #print axioms line in paper1/axiom_check.lean,\n                # and all are propext / Classical.choice / Quot.sound (python -m paper1.latex.check_axioms)\npython -m paper1.latex.check_lean      # every Lean name the draft cites is declared (run from the repo root)\npython -m pytest tests/test_paper1_latex.py -q\n```\n\n## Files\n\n| file | content | drafted from |\n|---|---|---|\n| `main.tex` | preamble, title, draft abstract | `../plan.md` |\n| `sec1_intro.tex` | 1 Introduction: MOSP, Table 1.1 (the Linhares–Yanasse table), how it came to be, contributions, notation | the first author's thesis; `../plan.md` §1 |\n| `sec2_equivalences.tex` | 2 The equivalences (was §3): the problems, Table 2.1 (master table), Figure 2.1 (`../figures/sec3_fig1_chain.pdf`, `python -m p