{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgbrPmzynATKLzM8nPBRZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Mu97xqCwtECTZQTMcm87bk","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'PYEOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('paper1/latex/sec9e_appendix_random.tex'); s=p.read_text()\n\n# 1. the ridge sentence of the generator subsection (plan, section 0, correction 1)\nold_ridge = r\"\"\"The classes of\nSection~\\ref{sec:results} that took thousands of core-hours to certify, the\nsparsest ones, have excess between $1.66$ and $1.78$, just inside the region\nwhere this argument proves linear pathwidth; on generated random covers the\ncost of the exact search peaks at excess about $2.3$ to $2.5$. Whether the\nthreshold and that peak are related is not known.\"\"\"\nnew_ridge = r\"\"\"The classes of\nSection~\\ref{sec:results} that took thousands of core-hours to certify, the\nsparsest ones ($d=2$), have excess between $1.66$ and $1.78$, just inside the\nregion where this argument proves linear pathwidth; on generated random covers\nthe cost of the exact search peaks at excess about $2$ to $2.4$, between the\n$d=2$ classes and the $d=4$ classes (excess $2.9$ to $3.4$ at 125 customers).\nSection~\\ref{app:excess} places every instance on that axis and tests, and\nrejects, the hypothesis that the peak is where this bound is loosest.\"\"\"\nassert old_ridge in s; s = s.replace(old_ridge, new_ridge)\n\n# 2. the coupling now has a Lean name\nold_c = r\"\"\"the repairs only add ones, so an empty pair of the padded table is an\nempty pair of the original and the bound with Bernoulli sizes holds for the\npadded instance;\"\"\"\nnew_c = r\"\"\"the repairs only add ones, so an empty pair of the padded table is an\nempty pair of the original~\\leanref{coupling} and the bound with Bernoulli\nsizes holds for the padded instance;\"\"\"\nassert old_c in s; s = s.replace(old_c, new_c)\n\n# 3. the new subsection, before \"What is checked, and what is known\"\nmarker = r\"\\subsection{What is checked, and what is known}\"\nassert marker in s\nsub = r\"\"\"% The excess subsection (loop0010 item 12, 2026-11-01). Source:\n% reports/excess_phase_diagram.md (the opening and sections 1-11); Lean:\n% ForestCover.lean, TreeBound.lean, IndependenceBound.lean. The figure is\n% paper1/figures/excess_phase_diagram.pdf from\n% paper1/latex/figures/excess_phase_diagram.py (its CSV is committed under\n% data/); the table is tables/excess_beta.tex from make_tables.py.\n\\subsection{The excess, and a phase diagram}\n\\label{app:excess}\n\nThe quantity that ends the previous subsection deserves a name and an axis.\nFor an instance with $n$ customers, $m$ patterns and $N$ ones in the table,\nthe \\emph{excess} is\n\\[\n  e \\;=\\; \\frac{N-m}{n},\n\\]\nthe mean number of patterns per customer minus one. With $r=m/n$ and $c=N/m$\nthe mean number of customers per pattern, $e=r(c-1)$; and if the\ncustomer--pattern incidence graph $B$, with a node for every customer and\nevery pattern and an edge for every one in the table, has $\\kappa$\ncomponents, its number of independent cycles is $n(e-1)+\\kappa$, so $B$ can\nbe a forest only when $e<1$. Throughout, $e$ is read from the table and never\nfrom a generator's nominal parameters, whose repairs inflate it. Three values\nof $e$ carry a meaning, and Figure~\\ref{fig:excess} places every instance of\nSection~\\ref{sec:results} against them.\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item $e=1$. The constant $\\beta^{*}(d,\\mu)$ of the previous subsection is\n  positive exactly when $\\mu(d-1)>1$, that is when $e>1$; proved on paper from\n  the exponent of the Lean inequality~\\leanref{clique-cover}.\n\\item $e$ from $2$ to $2.4$. On random covers of 15 to 75 customers, at every\n  ratio $m/n$ from $1/8$ to $2$, the nodes our search spends to refute\n  $\\mathrm{optimum}-1$ peak there, falling by two orders of magnitude on\n  either side (measured).\n\\item $e_g(r)=(\\sqrt{r^{2}+4r}-r)/2$, equal to $0.62$ at $m=n$. There the\n  branching factor of the random incidence graph with patterns of a fixed\n  size is one. On generated covers of 10 to 75 customers the share of\n  instances whose $B$ is a forest falls from $0.74$--$0.92$ below $e_g(r)$ to\n  $0.23$--$0.40$ between $e_g(r)$ and $1$, and is zero above $1$, as the\n  identity forces (measured).\n\\end{itemize}\nFour regimes follow. Below $e_g(r)$ the incidence graph is usually a forest\nand the open stacks are governed by the largest pattern\n(Theorem~\\ref{thm:blowup}). Between $e_g(r)$ and $1$ the giant component is\nthere but the first moment says nothing, $\\beta^{*}=0$, and the deterministic\nbounds of Section~\\ref{sec:results} are all there is. Between $1$ and about\n$2.4$ the first moment is positive, the certified optimum per customer sits\n$0.05$--$0.10$ above $\\beta^{*}$, and the cost of certification climbs to the\nridge; the $d=2$ classes of Section~\\ref{sec:results} sit at $e=1.66$--$1.78$,\non the sparse side of the ridge, and so do 23 of the 24 industrial instances.\nAbove the ridge the cost falls and $\\beta^{*}$ comes within $0.04$--$0.06$ of\nthe certified slope at $e\\ge6$ ($m=n$, 30--100 customers); the $d=4$ classes,\nat $e=2.9$--$3.7$, are the ridge's dense shoulder. No benchmark instance we\nhold has $e<e_g(r)$, and only $12$ of $6{,}376$ have $e<1$. The rest of this\nsubsection says what is proved and what is measured in each regime.\n\n\\paragraph{The forest regime.}\nWrite $\\omega$ for the size of the largest pattern.\n\\begin{theorem}[blow-up]\n\\label{thm:blowup}\nFor every instance, $\\mathrm{MOSP}\\le\\omega\\,(\\pw(B)+1)$. If $B$ is a forest,\n\\[\n  \\mathrm{MOSP}\\;\\le\\;\\omega\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\log_{3}\\tfrac{6(n+m)+3}{5}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\\end{theorem}\nTake a path decomposition of $B$ and replace each pattern node in each bag by\nthe pattern's customers. A customer's nodes in $B$ form a star, so the bags\nthat now contain the customer are still an interval, and each bag has grown\nby a factor of at most $\\omega$. Nothing about $B$ is used; the forest enters\nonly through the pathwidth of a forest on $N$ vertices, which is at most\n$\\lfloor\\log_{3}((6N+3)/5)\\rfloor$, the least $k$ with $N\\le(5\\cdot3^{k}-3)/2$,\nand that bound is sharp. Both statements are Lean\ntheorems~\\leanref{blowup}~\\leanref{forest}. The bound is loose. On 7,004\ngraphs whose pathwidth we computed exactly, clique trees to 936 vertices,\nrandom block trees to 120 customers, 3,448 certified random covers with a\nforest incidence graph and 2,000 small random instances, most of them not\nforests, the first inequality is never violated and never exact, and on the\nblock-graph families it is 2 to 5.5 times the truth; on the 3,448 random\nforests the pathwidth is $\\omega-1$ on $99.8\\%$ of them, so below $e_g(r)$ the\noptimum is, in practice, the largest pattern (measured). The exact theory is\nknown: node search on block graphs is polynomial\n\\citep{ChouKoHoChen2008}. On clique trees, one root clique $K_s$ with $b$\ncliques hung at every customer to depth $h$, the exact values are\nmultiplicative in $\\omega$ and the depth, about $(\\omega-2)/2$ per level, and\nno formula linear in $\\omega$ and $h$ fits them; a closed form we fitted to 29\nvalues failed on the 30th, and we conjecture none. A random corollary, that\nbelow $e_g(r)$ the optimum is $O(\\omega\\log\\log n)$ with high probability,\nneeds the size of the largest component of $B$, which we cite and do not\nprove.\n\n\\paragraph{Constructions from above.}\nThe first moment bounds $\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n$ from below. From above, two\nlayouts can be analysed.\n\\begin{lemma}[layers]\n\\label{lem:layer}\nIf $L_0,L_1,\\dots$ are the distance layers from any vertex of a connected\ngraph, $\\pw\\le\\max_i(|L_i|+|L_{i+1}|)-1$; and $\\pw\\le n-\\alpha$ for any\nindependent set of $\\alpha$ vertices.\n\\end{lemma}\nThe bags are $L_i\\cup L_{i+1}$ in the first case and $(V\\setminus I)\\cup\\{v\\}$\nfor $v\\in I$ in the second; both are Lean theorems~\\leanref{layer}~\\leanref{indep}.\nOn the uniform cover model with $m=\\mu n$ patterns of size $d$, the layer\nsizes follow an exploration recursion with two types of node, and the greedy\nindependent set that always takes the customer with fewest patterns follows a\ndifferential equation; the smaller of their two limits is a constant\n$\\beta^{+}(d,\\mu)$, derived on paper up to the concentration step, which we\ncite and do not re-prove. The layer layout wins below $e=1$, where the giant\ncomponent is small, and the greedy from $e=1.5$ on. Table~\\ref{tab:excess} gives\n$\\beta^{+}$ beside $\\beta^{*}$ and the slope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ fitted\nto the certified instances at 30--100 customers. The sandwich\n$\\beta^{*}\\le\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n\\le\\limsup\\mathrm{opt}/n\\le\\beta^{+}$ has\nwidth below $0.12$ at $e\\ge14$ and $0.42$--$0.49$ for $1\\le e\\le3.5$: it says\nleast exactly where the search works hardest. The greedy closing rule we use\nfor upper bounds, which closes the customer that opens fewest stacks, cannot\nbe analysed this way; at 5,000 customers its width per customer sits\n$0.06$--$0.36$ below $\\beta^{+}$ on the cells where its extrapolation agrees\nwith the certified slope (measured, 100--5,000 customers, no exact solve).\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Random covers with $m=n$ patterns of $d$ customers: the excess, the\nfirst-moment constant (a lower bound on $\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n$, proved), the\nslope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ fitted to 600--625 certified instances at\n30--100 customers (measured; one standard error in parentheses), and the\nconstant of the analysed layouts (an upper bound on $\\limsup\\mathrm{opt}/n$,\nproved on paper up to concentration).}\n\\label{tab:excess}\n\\small\n\\input{tables/excess_beta}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Why the first moment is loose, and what does not fix it.}\nA layout of width $k$ forces not one empty pair but $\\ell$ nested ones, one at\neach of $\\ell$ prefix sizes. Counting that structure with the exact\ninclusion--exclusion factor per pattern gives a constant $\\beta^{*}_{\\ell}$\nthat is \\emph{lower} than $\\beta^{*}$ on every cell we computed, by $0.051$\nat $\\ell=2$ for $d=6$, $\\mu=1$, and lower still for $\\ell=3,4$, at every\nspacing of the pairs: naming an extra cell of $\\varepsilon n$ vertices costs\n$\\Theta(\\varepsilon\\log(1/\\varepsilon))$ of entropy per customer and buys\n$\\Theta(\\varepsilon)$ of constraint. The slack is in the union bound. The\nempty pairs cluster, $E[X^{2}]/E[X]^{2}=e^{0.23n}$ at $\\beta^{*}$ for $d=6$,\n$\\mu=1$, and on 650 certified instances at 30--75 customers the empty-pair\ncondition itself is within a stack of the pathwidth while the floor sits one\nto three stacks lower. A second moment bounds $\\Pr[X>0]$ from the wrong side;\na count over one representative per cluster of pairs is the route we have not\ntaken.\n\n\\paragraph{One parameter, or two.}\nThe excess sets the threshold, the ridge and the scale, not the value. At\nequal excess the certified slope falls as the patterns get smaller and more\nnumerous: at $e=2$ it is $0.282$, $0.232$, $0.200$ and $0.167$ for\n$\\mu=1/4,1/2,1,2$, with standard errors $0.002$--$0.006$, and 13 of the 16\npairs of series matched in $e$ to $\\pm0.05$ differ by more than twice their\nerror. The first moment has the same dependence, $0.180$, $0.169$, $0.147$,\n$0.123$ at those four cells, and over 41 series $b=0.057+0.984\\,\\beta^{*}$\nwith a residual of $0.024$: $\\beta^{*}(d,\\mu)$ is the better one-parameter\ncoordinate, and $e$ is the coordinate of the lines. The ridge is not where\nthe first moment is loosest. On 25 series at 50--75 customers the gap between\nthe certified optimum and the floor peaks at $e=2.2$--$5.3$, on the dense\nside, with the ridge sitting on a plateau within one stack of that maximum on\nevery series. What peaks on the ridge is the number of closed sets the search\ncan reach by feasible steps, not the slack of the bound.\n\n\\paragraph{Twenty-four industrial instances.}\nThe 24 SCOOP instances \\citep{Frinhani2018}, from two sawmilling companies,\nhave excess $0.97$ to $2.50$, median $1.35$; 19 lie in $[1,2)$, three on the\nridge, one above, one between $e_g(r)$ and $1$; none has a forest incidence\ngraph and ten decompose into independent components. Every refutation of\n$\\mathrm{optimum}-1$ on record for them took at most $22{,}433$ nodes, and the\ngreedy closing rule is exact on 12 of the 24 and at most $0.061n$ above the\noptimum. One instance, with 60 customers, 49 patterns and optimum 7, sits two\nstacks below the first-moment floor that a random cover with its pattern\nsizes would have at confidence $0.01$: a random cover of that size law has\noptimum at most 7 with probability below $7\\times10^{-5}$. That is not a\ncontradiction, the floor being a statement about the random model; it is a\nmeasurement of distance from random, and the designed instances of the 2005\nChallenge sit up to 39 stacks below theirs. These are 24 instances from one\nsource, read with pieces as customers and patterns as patterns, the\norientation of the files' format line and the opposite of their description's\nprose; we say nothing about industry beyond them.\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{excess_phase_diagram.pdf}\n\\caption{The excess phase diagram. Above, where the instances of\nSection~\\ref{sec:results} sit: the 24 SCOOP instances; the Chu \\& Stuckey\nclasses with $m=n$ as the median of each density over all sizes; the median\nand quartiles of the other collections. The dashed line is $e_g(1)=0.62$, the\nsolid line $e=1$, the band $e=2$--$2.4$ the ridge. Below, random covers with\n$m=n$ patterns of $d$ customers, $d=2$ to $10$: the first-moment constant\n$\\beta^{*}$ (a theorem), the slope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ on certified\ninstances at 30--100 customers with 