two.}\nThe excess sets the threshold, the ridge and the scale, not the value. At\nequal excess the certified slope falls as the patterns get smaller and more\nnumerous: at $e=2$ it is $0.282$, $0.232$, $0.200$ and $0.167$ for\n$\\mu=1/4,1/2,1,2$, with standard errors $0.002$--$0.006$, and 13 of the 16\npairs of series matched in $e$ to $\\pm0.05$ differ by more than twice their\nerror. The first moment has the same dependence, $0.180$, $0.169$, $0.147$,\n$0.123$ at those four cells, and over 41 series $b=0.057+0.984\\,\\beta^{*}$\nwith a residual of $0.024$: $\\beta^{*}(d,\\mu)$ is the better one-parameter\ncoordinate, and $e$ is the coordinate of the lines. The ridge is not where\nthe first moment is loosest. On 25 series at 50--75 customers the gap between\nthe certified optimum and the floor peaks at $e=2.2$--$5.3$, on the dense\nside, with the ridge sitting on a plateau within one stack of that maximum on\nevery series. What peaks on the ridge is the number of closed sets the search\ncan reach by feasible steps, not the slack of the bound.\n\n\\paragraph{Twenty-four industrial instances.}\nThe 24 SCOOP instances \\citep{Frinhani2018}, from two sawmilling companies,\nhave excess $0.97$ to $2.50$, median $1.35$; 19 lie in $[1,2)$, three on the\nridge, one above, one between $e_g(r)$ and $1$; none has a forest incidence\ngraph and ten decompose into independent components. Every refutation of\n$\\mathrm{optimum}-1$ on record for them took at most $22{,}433$ nodes, and the\ngreedy closing rule is exact on 12 of the 24 and at most $0.061n$ above the\noptimum. One instance, with 60 customers, 49 patterns and optimum 7, sits two\nstacks below the first-moment floor that a random cover with its pattern\nsizes would have at confidence $0.01$: a random cover of that size law has\noptimum at most 7 with probability below $7\\times10^{-5}$. That is not a\ncontradiction, the floor being a statement about the random model; it is a\nmeasurement of distance from random, and the