{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Mu97xqCwtECTZQTMcm87bk","type":"tool_result","content":"sec9e updated","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b64b2670-263a-4912-8a4a-d8fc3e1ebe26","uuid":"511af1e7-ee91-47d2-854e-0e07d744f830","timestamp":"2026-11-01T07:58:54.405Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"sec9e updated","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper1/latex/sec9e_appendix_random.tex","hunks":[{"oldStart":195,"oldLines":10,"newStart":195,"newLines":12,"lines":[" customer minus one, the number of ones in the table minus the number of"," patterns, per customer; call it the excess. The classes of"," Section~\\ref{sec:results} that took thousands of core-hours to certify, the","-sparsest ones, have excess between $1.66$ and $1.78$, just inside the region","-where this argument proves linear pathwidth; on generated random covers the","-cost of the exact search peaks at excess about $2.3$ to $2.5$. Whether the","-threshold and that peak are related is not known.","+sparsest ones ($d=2$), have excess between $1.66$ and $1.78$, just inside the","+region where this argument proves linear pathwidth; on generated random covers","+the cost of the exact search peaks at excess about $2$ to $2.4$, between the","+$d=2$ classes and the $d=4$ classes (excess $2.9$ to $3.4$ at 125 customers).","+Section~\\ref{app:excess} places every instance on that axis and tests, and","+rejects, the hypothesis that the peak is where this bound is loosest."," "," \\begin{table}[htbp]"," \\centering"]},{"oldStart":217,"oldLines":8,"newStart":219,"newLines":8,"lines":[" pair is empty with probability exactly"," $[(1-p)^t+(1-p)^{t-1}-(1-p)^{2t-1}]^m$, the binomial average of the fraction"," above; the repairs only add ones, so an empty pair of the padded table is an","-empty pair of the original and the bound with Bernoulli sizes holds for the","-padded instance; and if the patterns are padded before the customers, the","+empty pair of the original~\\leanref{coupling} and the bound with Bernoulli","+sizes holds for the padded instance; and if the patterns are padded before the customers, the"," rows after that pad are independent uniform sets of size $\\max(s,2)$ with $s$"," binomial, and the bound holds with those sizes. The rejection is not an"," increasing event of the table entries: adding an entry can make a pad vanish,"]},{"oldStart":250,"oldLines":6,"newStart":252,"newLines":202,"lines":[" linear; his threshold of two patterns per customer is the dropped factor's"," artefact, and with it restored the condition at $m=n$ is about $2.46$."," ","+% The excess subsection (loop0010 item 12, 2026-11-01). Source:","+% reports/excess_phase_diagram.md (the opening and sections 1-11); Lean:","+% ForestCover.lean, TreeBound.lean, IndependenceBound.lean. The figure is","+% paper1/figures/excess_phase_diagram.pdf from","+% paper1/latex/figures/excess_phase_diagram.py (its CSV is committed under","+% data/); the table is tables/excess_beta.tex from make_tables.py.","+\\subsection{The excess, and a phase diagram}","+\\label{app:excess}","+","+The quantity that ends the previous subsection deserves a name and an axis.","+For an instance with $n$ customers, $m$ patterns and $N$ ones in the table,","+the \\emph{excess} is","+\\[","+  e \\;=\\; \\frac{N-m}{n},","+\\]","+the mean number of patterns per customer minus one. With $r=m/n$ and $c=N/m$","+the mean number of customers per pattern, $e=r(c-1)$; and if the","+customer--pattern incidence graph $B$, with a node for every customer and","+every pattern and an edge for every one in the table, has $\\kappa$","+components, its number of independent cycles is $n(e-1)+\\kappa$, so $B$ can","+be a forest only when $e<1$. Throughout, $e$ is read from the table and never","+from a generator's nominal parameters, whose repairs inflate it. Three values","+of $e$ carry a meaning, and Figure~\\ref{fig:excess} places every instance of","+Section~\\ref{sec:results} against them.","+\\begin{itemize}","+\\item $e=1$. The constant $\\beta^{*}(d,\\mu)$ of the previous subsection is","+  positive exactly when $\\mu(d-1)>1$, that is when $e>1$; proved on paper from","+  the exponent of the Lean inequality~\\leanref{clique-cover}.","+\\item $e$ from $2$ to $2.4$. On random covers of 15 to 75 customers, at every","+  ratio $m/n$ from $1/8$ to $2$, the nodes our search spends to refute","+  $\\mathrm{optimum}-1$ peak there, falling by two orders of magnitude on","+  either side (measured).","+\\item $e_g(r)=(\\sqrt{r^{2}+4r}-r)/2$, equal to $0.62$ at $m=n$. There the","+  branching factor of the random incidence graph with patterns of a fixed","+  size is one. On generated covers of 10 to 75 customers the share of","+  instances whose $B$ is a forest falls from $0.74$--$0.92$ below $e_g(r)$ to","+  $0.23$--$0.40$ between $e_g(r)$ and $1$, and is zero above $1$, as the","+  identity forces (measured).","+\\end{itemize}","+Four regimes follow. Below $e_g(r)$ the incidence graph is usually a forest","+and the open stacks are governed by the largest pattern","+(Theorem~\\ref{thm:blowup}). Between $e_g(r)$ and $1$ the giant component is","+there but the first moment says nothing, $\\beta^{*}=0$, and the deterministic","+bounds of Section~\\ref{sec:results} are all there is. Between $1$ and about","+$2.4$ the first moment is positive, the certified optimum per customer sits","+$0.05$--$0.10$ above $\\beta^{*}$, and the cost of certification climbs to the","+ridge; the $d=2$ classes of Section~\\ref{sec:results} sit at $e=1.66$--$1.78$,","+on the sparse side of the ridge, and so do 23 of the 24 industrial instances.","+Above the ridge the cost falls and $\\beta^{*}$ comes within $0.04$--$0.06$ of","+the certified slope at $e\\ge6$ ($m=n$, 30--100 customers); the $d=4$ classes,","+at $e=2.9$--$3.7$, are the ridge's dense shoulder. No benchmark instance we","+hold has $e<e_g(r)$, and only $12$ of $6{,}376$ have $e<1$. The rest of this","+subsection says what is proved and what is measured in each regime.","+","+\\paragraph{The forest regime.}","+Write $\\omega$ for the size of the largest pattern.","+\\begin{theorem}[blow-up]","+\\label{thm:blowup}","+For every instance, $\\mathrm{MOSP}\\le\\omega\\,(\\pw(B)+1)$. If $B$ is a forest,","+\\[","+  \\mathrm{MOSP}\\;\\le\\;\\omega\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\log_{3}\\tfrac{6(n+m)+3}{5}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr).","+\\]","+\\end{theorem}","+Take a path decomposition of $B$ and replace each pattern node in each bag by","+the pattern's customers. A customer's nodes in $B$ form a star, so the bags","+that now contain the customer are still an interval, and each bag has grown","+by a factor of at most $\\omega$. Nothing about $B$ is used; the forest enters","+only through the pathwidth of a forest on $N$ vertices, which is at most","+$\\lfloor\\log_{3}((6N+3)/5)\\rfloor$, the least $k$ with $N\\le(5\\cdot3^{k}-3)/2$,","+and that bound is sharp. Both statements are Lean","+theorems~\\leanref{blowup}~\\leanref{forest}. The bound is loose. On 7,004","+graphs whose pathwidth we computed exactly, clique trees to 936 vertices,","+random block trees to 120 customers, 3,448 certified random covers with a","+forest incidence graph and 2,000 small random instances, most of them not","+forests, the first inequality is never violated and never exact, and on the","+block-graph families it is 2 to 5.5 times the truth; on the 3,448 random","+forests the pathwidth is $\\omega-1$ on $99.8\\%$ of them, so below $e_g(r)$ the","+optimum is, in practice, the largest pattern (measured). The exact theory is","+known: node search on block graphs is polynomial","+\\citep{ChouKoHoChen2008}. On clique trees, one root clique $K_s$ with $b$","+cliques hung at every customer to depth $h$, the exact values are","+multiplicative in $\\omega$ and the depth, about $(\\omega-2)/2$ per level, and","+no formula linear in $\\omega$ and $h$ fits them; a closed form we fitted to 29","+values failed on the 30th, and we conjecture none. A random corollary, that","+below $e_g(r)$ the optimum is $O(\\omega\\log\\log n)$ with high probability,","+needs the size of the largest component of $B$, which we cite and do not","+prove.","+","+\\paragraph{Constructions from above.}","+The first moment bounds $\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n$ from below. From above, two","+layouts can be analysed.","+\\begin{lemma}[layers]","+\\label{lem:layer}","+If $L_0,L_1,\\dots$ are the distance layers from any vertex of a connected","+graph, $\\pw\\le\\max_i(|L_i|+|L_{i+1}|)-1$; and $\\pw\\le n-\\alpha$ for any","+independent set of $\\alpha$ vertices.","+\\end{lemma}","+The bags are $L_i\\cup L_{i+1}$ in the first case and $(V\\setminus I)\\cup\\{v\\}$","+for $v\\in I$ in the second; both are Lean theorems~\\leanref{layer}~\\leanref{indep}.","+On the uniform cover model with $m=\\mu n$ patterns of size $d$, the layer","+sizes follow an exploration recursion with two types of node, and the greedy","+independent set that always takes the customer with fewest patterns follows a","+differential equation; the smaller of their two limits is a constant","+$\\beta^{+}(d,\\mu)$, derived on paper up to the concentration step, which we","+cite and do not re-prove. The layer layout wins below $e=1$, where the giant","+component is small, and the greedy from $e=1.5$ on. Table~\\ref{tab:excess} gives","+$\\beta^{+}$ beside $\\beta^{*}$ and the slope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ fitted","+to the certified instances at 30--100 customers. The sandwich","+$\\beta^{*}\\le\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n\\le\\limsup\\mathrm{opt}/n\\le\\beta^{+}$ has","+width below $0.12$ at $e\\ge14$ and $0.42$--$0.49$ for $1\\le e\\le3.5$: it says","+least exactly where the search works hardest. The greedy closing rule we use","+for upper bounds, which closes the customer that opens fewest stacks, cannot","+be analysed this way; at 5,000 customers its width per customer sits","+$0.06$--$0.36$ below $\\beta^{+}$ on the cells where its extrapolation agrees","+with the certified slope (measured, 100--5,000 customers, no exact solve).","+","+\\begin{table}[htbp]","+\\centering","+\\caption{Random covers with $m=n$ patterns of $d$ customers: the excess, the","+first-moment constant (a lower bound on $\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n$, proved), the","+slope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ fitted to 600--625 certified instances at","+30--100 customers (measured; one standard error in parentheses), and the","+constant of the analysed layouts (an upper bound on $\\limsup\\mathrm{opt}/n$,","+proved on paper up to concentration).}","+\\label{tab:excess}","+\\small","+\\input{tables/excess_beta}","+\\end{table}","+","+\\paragraph{Why the first moment is loose, and what does not fix it.}","+A layout of width $k$ forces not one empty pair but $\\ell$ nested ones, one at","+each of $\\ell$ prefix sizes. Counting that structure with the exact","+inclusion--exclusion factor per pattern gives a constant $\\beta^{*}_{\\ell}$","+that is \\emph{lower} than $\\beta^{*}$ on every cell we computed, by $0.051$","+at $\\ell=2$ for $d=6$, $\\mu=1$, and lower still for $\\ell=3,4$, at every","+spacing of the pairs: naming an extra cell of $\\varepsilon n$ vertices costs","+$\\Theta(\\varepsilon\\log(1/\\varepsilon))$ of entropy per customer and buys","+$\\Theta(\\varepsilon)$ of constraint. The slack is in the union bound. The","+empty pairs cluster, $E[X^{2}]/E[X]^{2}=e^{0.23n}$ at $\\beta^{*}$ for $d=6$,","+$\\mu=1$, and on 650 certified instances at 30--75 customers the empty-pair","+condition itself is within a stack of the pathwidth while the floor sits one","+to three stacks lower. A second moment bounds $\\Pr[X>0]$ from the wrong side;","+a count over one representative per cluster of pairs is the route we have not","+taken.","+","+\\paragraph{One parameter, or two.}","+The excess sets the threshold, the ridge and the scale, not the value. At","+equal excess the certified slope falls as the patterns get smaller and more","+numerous: at $e=2$ it is $0.282$, $0.232$, $0.200$ and $0.167$ for","+$\\mu=1/4,1/2,1,2$, with standard errors $0.002$--$0.006$, and 13 of the 16","+pairs of series matched in $e$ to $\\pm0.05$ differ by more than twice their","+error. The first moment has the same dependence, $0.180$, $0.169$, $0.147$,","+$0.123$ at those four cells, and over 41 series $b=0.057+0.984\\,\\beta^{*}$","+with a residual of $0.024$: $\\beta^{*}(d,\\mu)$ is the better one-parameter","+coordinate, and $e$ is the coordinate of the lines. The ridge is not where","+the first moment is loosest. On 25 series at 50--75 customers the gap between","+the certified optimum and the floor peaks at $e=2.2$--$5.3$, on the dense","+side, with the ridge sitting on a plateau within one stack of that maximum on","+every series. What peaks on the ridge is the number of closed sets the search","+can reach by feasible steps, not the slack of the bound.","+","+\\paragraph{Twenty-four industrial instances.}","+The 24 SCOOP instances \\citep{Frinhani2018}, from two sawmilling companies,","+have excess $0.97$ to $2.50$, median $1.35$; 19 lie in $[1,2)$, three on the","+ridge, one above, one between $e_g(r)$ and $1$; none has a forest incidence","+graph and ten decompose into independent components. Every refutation of","+$\\mathrm{optimum}-1$ on record for them took at most $22{,}433$ nodes, and the","+greedy closing rule is exact on 12 of the 24 and at most $0.061n$ above the","+optimum. One instance, with 60 customers, 49 patterns and optimum 7, sits two","+stacks below the first-moment floor that a random cover with its pattern","+sizes would have at confidence $0.01$: a random cover of that size law has","+optimum at most 7 with probability below $7\\times10^{-5}$. That is not a","+contradiction, the floor being a statement about the random model; it is a","+measurement of distance from random, and the designed instances of the 2005","+Challenge sit up to 39 stacks below theirs. These are 24 instances from one","+source, read with pieces as customers and patterns as patterns, the","+orientation of the files' format line and the opposite of their description's","+prose; we say nothing about industry beyond them.","+","+\\begin{figure}[htbp]","+\\centering","+\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{excess_phase_diagram.pdf}","+\\caption{The excess phase diagram. Above, where the instances of","+Section~\\ref{sec:results} sit: the 24 SCOOP instances; the Chu \\& Stuckey","+classes with $m=n$ as the median of each density over all sizes; the median","+and quartiles of the other collections. The dashed line is $e_g(1)=0.62$, the","+solid line $e=1$, the band $e=2$--$2.4$ the ridge. Below, random covers with","+$m=n$ patterns of $d$ customers, $d=2$ to $10$: the first-moment constant","+$\\beta^{*}$ (a theorem), the slope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ on certified","+instances at 30--100 customers with its two-standard-error bar (measured), and","+the constant $\\beta^{+}$ of the analysed layouts; in grey, $b$ at $m=n/2$ and","+$m=2n$, which differ from it at equal excess. Regenerate:","+\\repofile{paper1/latex/figures/excess_phase_diagram.py}.}","+\\label{fig:excess}","+\\end{figure}","+"," \\subsection{What is checked, and what is known}"," "," Steps 1, 2, 3 and 4 and the two inequalities of Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment}"]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper1/latex/sec9e_appendix_random.tex"]}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Mu97xqCwtECTZQTMcm87bk","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgbrPmzynATKLzM8nPBRZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HLAqVqJqad4YxymB8U58jT","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat >> tests/test_paper1_latex.py <<'PYEOF'\n\n\n# --- Appendix E, the excess phase diagram (loop0010 item 12) -----------------\n\ndef _phase_module():\n    import importlib.util\n    path = HERE / \"figures\" / \"excess_phase_diagram.py\"\n    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"excess_phase_diagram\", path)\n    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\n    spec.loader.exec_module(module)\n    return module\n\n\ndef _excess_of_files(directory: Path) -> dict[str, tuple[int, int, float]]:\n    \"\"\"(n, m, e) per instance name, read straight from the benchmark files.\"\"\"\n    import numpy as np\n    from learning.excess import excess\n    from mosp.instance import MOSPInstance\n    out = {}\n    for f in sorted(directory.glob(\"*.txt\")):\n        try:\n            instances = MOSPInstance.from_benchmark_file(f)\n        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - the dataset description file\n            continue\n        for inst in instances:\n            matrix = np.asarray(inst.matrix)\n            n, m = matrix.shape\n            out[inst.name] = (n, m, excess(n, m, int(matrix.sum())))\n    return out\n\n\ndef test_phase_diagram_csv_matches_its_sources():\n    \"\"\"The committed CSV behind Figure E.1 and Table E.3: the beta+ rows equal\n    the tracked table of item 07; the SCOOP and Chu & Stuckey rows equal the\n    excess recomputed from the benchmark files; the series rows equal\n    `learning.excess.series_slopes` where its (git-ignored) input exists.\"\"\"\n    import pandas as pd\n    mod = _phase_module()\n    data = mod.load_data()\n    assert set(data[\"kind\"]) == {\"collection\", \"scoop\", \"class\", \"series\", \"beta_plus\"}\n\n    plus = pd.read_csv(mod.BETA_PLUS_CSV)\n    rows = data[data[\"kind\"] == \