{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q4sLbuwV5PoT3fe3KWvLg2","type":"tool_result","content":"=========== page 67\nE.4   The excess, and a phase diagram\nThe quantity that ends the previous subsection deserves a name and an axis. For an instance\nwith n customers, m patterns and N ones in the table, the excess is\n\n                                                  N−m\n                                            e =       ,\n                                                   n\nthe mean number of patterns per customer minus one. With r = m/n and c = N/m the mean\nnumber of customers per pattern, e = r (c − 1); and if the customer–pattern incidence graph B,\nwith a node for every customer and every pattern and an edge for every one in the table, has\nκ components, its number of independent cycles is n(e − 1) + κ, so B can be a forest only when\ne < 1. Throughout, e is read from the table and never from a generator’s nominal parameters,\nwhose repairs inflate it. Three values of e carry a meaning, and Figure E.1 places every instance\nof Section 5 against them.\n\n   • e = 1. The constant β∗ (d, µ) of the previous subsection is positive exactly when µ(d − 1) >\n     1, that is when e > 1; proved on paper from the exponent of the Lean inequality (Ap-\n     pendix A, A.56).\n\n   • e from 2 to 2.4. On random covers of 15 to 75 customers, at every ratio m/n from 1/8 to\n     2, the nodes our search spends to refute optimum − 1 peak there, falling by two orders of\n     magnitude on either side (measured).\n                 √\n   • e g (r ) = ( r2 + 4r − r )/2, equal to 0.62 at m = n. There the branching factor of the random\n     incidence graph with patterns of a fixed size is one. On generated covers of 10 to 75\n     customers the share of instances whose B is a forest falls from 0.74–0.92 below e g (r ) to\n     0.23–0.40 between e g (r ) and 1, and is zero above 1, as the identity forces (measured).\n\nFour regimes follow. Below e g (r ) the incidence graph is usually a forest and the open stacks\nare governed by the largest pattern (Theorem E.3). Between e g (r ) and 1 the giant component\nis there but the first moment says nothing, β∗ = 0, and the deterministic bounds of Section 5\nare all there is. Between 1 and about 2.4 the first moment is positive, the certified optimum per\ncustomer sits 0.05–0.10 above β∗ , and the cost of certification climbs to the ridge; the d = 2 classes\nof Section 5 sit at e = 1.66–1.78, on the sparse side of the ridge, and so do 23 of the 24 industrial\ninstances. Above the ridge the cost falls and β∗ comes within 0.04–0.06 of the certified slope at\ne ≥ 6 (m = n, 30–100 customers); the d = 4 classes, at e = 2.9–3.7, are the ridge’s dense shoulder.\nNo benchmark instance we hold has e < e g (r ), and only 12 of 6,376 have e < 1. The rest of this\nsubsection says what is proved and what is measured in each regime.\n\nThe forest regime.    Write ω for the size of the largest pattern.\n\nTheorem E.3 (blow-up). For every instance, MOSP ≤ ω (pw( B) + 1). If B is a forest,\n                                           \u0010j            k   \u0011\n                                                6(n+m)+3\n                             MOSP ≤ ω log3          5      +1 .\n\n   Take a path decomposition of B and replace each pattern node in each bag by the pattern’s\ncustomers. A customer’s nodes in B form a star, so the bags that now contain the customer\nare still an interval, and each bag has grown by a factor of at most ω. Nothing about B is\n\n                                                  67\n\f=========== page 68\nused; the forest enters only through the pathwidth of a forest on N vertices, which is at most\n⌊log3 ((6N + 3)/5)⌋, the least k with N ≤ (5 · 3k − 3)/2, and that bound is sharp. Both statements\nare Lean theorems (Appendix A, A.58) (Appendix A, A.59). The bound is loose. On 7,004 graphs\nwhose pathwidth we computed exactly, clique trees to 936 vertices, random block trees to 120\ncustomers, 3,448 certified random covers with a forest incidence graph and 2,000 small random\ninstances, most of them not forests, the first inequality is never violated and never exact, and on\nthe block-graph families it is 2 to 5.5 times the truth; on the 3,448 random forests the pathwidth is\nω − 1 on 99.8% of them, so below e g (r ) the optimum is, in practice, the largest pattern (measured).\nThe exact theory is known: node search on block graphs is polynomial (Chou et al., 2008). On\nclique trees, one root clique Ks with b cliques hung at every customer to depth h, the exact values\nare multiplicative in ω and the depth, about (ω − 2)/2 per level, and no formula linear in ω and\nh fits them; a closed form we fitted to 29 values failed on the 30th, and we conjecture none. A\nrandom corollary, that below e g (r ) the optimum is O(ω log log n) with high probability, needs\nthe size of the largest component of B, which we cite and do not prove.\n\nConstructions from above. The first moment bounds lim inf opt/n from below. From above,\ntwo layouts can be analysed.\nLemma E.4 (layers). If L0 , L1 , . . . are the distance layers from any vertex of a connected graph, pw ≤\nmaxi (| Li | + | Li+1 |) − 1; and pw ≤ n − α for any independent set of α vertices.\n    The bags are Li ∪ Li+1 in the first case and (V \\ I ) ∪ {v} for v ∈ I in the second; both are\nLean theorems (Appendix A, A.61) (Appendix A, A.62). On the uniform cover model with\nm = µn patterns of size d, the layer sizes follow an exploration recursion with two types of node,\nand the greedy independent set that always takes the customer with fewest patterns follows a\ndifferential equation; the smaller of their two limits is a constant β+ (d, µ), derived on paper up\nto the concentration step, which we cite and do not re-prove. The layer layout wins below e = 1,\nwhere the giant component is small, and the greedy from e = 1.5 on. Table E.3 gives β+ beside\nβ∗ and the slope 