pairs: naming an extra cell of εn vertices costs Θ(ε log(1/ε)) of entropy per customer and buys\nΘ(ε) of constraint. The slack is in the union bound. The empty pairs cluster, E[ X 2 ]/E[ X ]2 = e0.23n\nat β∗ for d = 6, µ = 1, and on 650 certified instances at 30–75 customers the empty-pair condition\nitself is within a stack of the pathwidth while the floor sits one to three stacks lower. A second\nmoment bounds Pr[ X > 0] from the wrong side; a count over one representative per cluster of\npairs is the route we have not taken.\n\nOne parameter, or two. The excess sets the threshold, the ridge and the scale, not the value. At\nequal excess the certified slope falls as the patterns get smaller and more numerous: at e = 2\nit is 0.282, 0.232, 0.200 and 0.167 for µ = 1/4, 1/2, 1, 2, with standard errors 0.002–0.006, and 13\nof the 16 pairs of series matched in e to ±0.05 differ by more than twice their error. The first\nmoment has the same dependence, 0.180, 0.169, 0.147, 0.123 at those four cells, and over 41 series\nb = 0.057 + 0.984 β∗ with a residual of 0.024: β∗ (d, µ) is the better one-parameter coordinate, and\ne is the coordinate of the lines. The ridge is not where the first moment is loosest. On 25 series at\n50–75 customers the gap between the certified optimum and the floor peaks at e = 2.2–5.3, on the\ndense side, with the ridge sitting on a plateau within one stack of that maximum on every series.\nWhat peaks on the ridge is the number of closed sets the search can reach by feasible steps, not\nthe slack of the bound.\n\nTwenty-four industrial instances. The 24 SCOOP instances (Frinhani et al., 2018), from two\nsawmilling companies, have excess 0.97 to 2.50, median 1.35; 19 lie in [1, 2), three on the ridge,\none above, one between e g (r ) and 1; none has a forest incidence graph and ten decompose into\nindependent components. Every refutation of optimum − 1 on record for them took at most\n22,433 nodes, and the greedy closing rule is exact on 12 of the 