{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgbrcgXBLKX5KjFX9DUTH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016TPb8UAmPNAQLH5tKtbJ5R","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'PYEOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('paper1/latex/sec9e_appendix_random.tex'); s=p.read_text()\nstart = s.index(r\"\\subsection{The excess, and a phase diagram}\")\nend = s.index(r\"\\subsection{What is checked, and what is known}\")\nsub = r\"\"\"\\subsection{The excess, and a phase diagram}\n\\label{app:excess}\n\nThe quantity that ends the previous subsection deserves a name and an axis.\nFor an instance with $n$ customers, $m$ patterns and $N$ ones in the table,\nthe \\emph{excess} is $e=(N-m)/n$, the mean number of patterns per customer\nminus one. With $r=m/n$ and $c=N/m$ the mean customers per pattern,\n$e=r(c-1)$; and if the customer--pattern incidence graph $B$, with a node per\ncustomer and per pattern and an edge per one in the table, has $\\kappa$\ncomponents, it has $n(e-1)+\\kappa$ independent cycles, so $B$ can be a forest\nonly when $e<1$. We read $e$ from the table, never from a generator's nominal\nparameters, whose repairs inflate it. Three values of $e$ carry a meaning.\nThe constant $\\beta^{*}(d,\\mu)$ is positive exactly when $\\mu(d-1)>1$, that\nis when $e>1$ (on paper, from the exponent of~\\leanref{clique-cover}). On\nrandom covers of 15 to 75 customers at every ratio $m/n$ from $1/8$ to $2$,\nthe nodes our search spends to refute $\\mathrm{optimum}-1$ peak at $e=2$ to\n$2.4$, falling by two orders of magnitude on either side (measured). And at\n$e_g(r)=(\\sqrt{r^{2}+4r}-r)/2$, $0.62$ for $m=n$, the branching factor of the\nrandom incidence graph is one: on generated covers of 10 to 75 customers the\nshare of instances whose $B$ is a forest falls from $0.74$--$0.92$ below\n$e_g(r)$ to $0.23$--$0.40$ between $e_g(r)$ and $1$, and is zero above\n(measured). Figure~\\ref{fig:excess} places every instance of\nSection~\\ref{sec:results} against the three. Below $e_g(r)$ the open stacks\nare governed by the largest pattern (Theorem~\\ref{thm:blowup}); between\n$e_g(r)$ and $1$ the first moment says nothing and the deterministic bounds\nare all there is; between $1$ and the ridge the certified optimum per\ncustomer sits $0.05$--$0.10$ above $\\beta^{*}$ and the cost of certification\nclimbs, and this is where the $d=2$ classes of Section~\\ref{sec:results}\n($e=1.66$--$1.78$) and 23 of the 24 industrial instances sit; above the ridge\nthe cost falls and $\\beta^{*}$ comes within $0.04$--$0.06$ of the certified\nslope at $e\\ge6$ ($m=n$, 30--100 customers), the $d=4$ classes\n($e=2.9$--$3.7$) being the dense shoulder. No benchmark instance we hold has\n$e<e_g(r)$, and 12 of $6{,}376$ have $e<1$.\n\n\\paragraph{The forest regime.}\nWrite $\\omega$ for the size of the largest pattern.\n\\begin{theorem}[blow-up]\n\\label{thm:blowup}\nFor every instance, $\\mathrm{MOSP}\\le\\omega\\,(\\pw(B)+1)$. If $B$ is a forest,\n$\\mathrm{MOSP}\\le\\omega\\bigl(\\lfloor\\log_{3}\\tfrac{6(n+m)+3}{5}\\rfloor+1\\bigr)$.\n\\end{theorem}\nReplace each pattern node in each bag of a path decomposition of $B$ by the\npattern's customers: a customer's nodes in $B$ form a star, so its bags stay\nan interval, and no bag grows by more than a factor $\\omega$. Nothing about\n$B$ is used; the forest enters only through the pathwidth of a forest on $N$\nvertices, at most $\\lfloor\\log_{3}((6N+3)/5)\\rfloor$, which is sharp. Both are\nLean theorems~\\leanref{blowup}~\\leanref{forest}. The bound is loose: on 7,004\ngraphs with exact pathwidth (clique trees to 936 vertices, random block trees,\n3,448 certified random covers whose $B$ is a forest, and 2,000 small random\ninstances) it is never violated and never exact, 2 to 5.5 times the truth on\nthe block-graph families; on the 3,448 random forests the pathwidth is\n$\\omega-1$ on $99.8\\%$, so below $e_g(r)$ the optimum is, in practice, the\nlargest pattern (measured). The exact theory on block graphs is polynomial\n\\citep{ChouKoHoChen2008}; on clique trees the exact values grow by about\n$(\\omega-2)/2$ per level of depth, no formula linear in $\\omega$ and the depth\nfits them, and we conjecture no closed form.\n\n\\paragraph{Constructions from above.}\n\\begin{lemma}[layers]\n\\label{lem:layer}\nIf $L_0,L_1,\\dots$ are the distance layers from any vertex of a connected\ngraph, $\\pw\\le\\max_i(|L_i|+|L_{i+1}|)-1$; and $\\pw\\le n-\\alpha$ for any\nindependent set of $\\alpha$ vertices.\n\\end{lemma}\nThe bags are $L_i\\cup L_{i+1}$, and $(V\\setminus I)\\cup\\{v\\}$ for $v\\in\nI$~\\leanref{layer}~\\leanref{indep}. On the uniform cover model with $m=\\mu n$\npatterns of size $d$ the layer sizes follow a two-type exploration recursion\nand the greedy independent set that takes the customer with fewest patterns\nfollows a differential equation; the smaller limit is a constant\n$\\beta^{+}(d,\\mu)$, derived on paper up to the concentration step, which we\ncite and do not re-prove. Table~\\ref{tab:excess} gives it beside $\\beta^{*}$\nand the slope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ on the certified instances at\n30--100 customers. The sandwich\n$\\beta^{*}\\le\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n\\le\\limsup\\mathrm{opt}/n\\le\\beta^{+}$ has\nwidth below $0.12$ at $e\\ge14$ and $0.42$--$0.49$ for $1\\le e\\le3.5$: it says\nleast where the search works hardest. The greedy closing rule we use for\nupper bounds cannot be analysed this way; at 5,000 customers its width per\ncustomer is $0.06$--$0.36$ below $\\beta^{+}$ (measured, no exact solve).\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Random covers with $m=n$ patterns of $d$ customers: the excess, the\nfirst-moment constant (a proved lower bound on $\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n$), the\nslope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ on 600--625 certified instances at 30--100\ncustomers (measured; one standard error in parentheses), and the constant of\nthe analysed layouts (an upper bound on $\\limsup\\mathrm{opt}/n$, on paper up\nto concentration).}\n\\label{tab:excess}\n\\small\n\\input{tables/excess_beta}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Why the first moment is loose.}\nA layout of width $k$ forces $\\ell$ nested empty pairs, one per prefix size,\nbut counting them gives a constant \\emph{below} $\\beta^{*}$ on every cell (by\n$0.051$ at $\\ell=2$, $d=6$, $\\mu=1$, and more for larger $\\ell$, at every\nspacing): naming $\\varepsilon n$ more vertices costs\n$\\Theta(\\varepsilon\\log(1/\\varepsilon))$ of entropy and buys\n$\\Theta(\\varepsilon)$ of constraint. The slack is in the union bound. The\nempty pairs cluster ($E[X^{2}]/E[X]^{2}=e^{0.23n}$ at $\\beta^{*}$ for $d=6$,\n$\\mu=1$), and on 650 certified instances at 30--75 customers the empty-pair\ncondition is within a stack of the pathwidth while the floor sits one to\nthree stacks lower. A second moment bounds $\\Pr[X>0]$ from the wrong side; a\ncount over one representative per cluster is the route not taken.\n\n\\paragraph{One parameter, or two.}\nThe excess sets the threshold, the ridge and the scale, not the value. At\n$e=2$ the certified slope is $0.282$, $0.232$, $0.200$, $0.167$ for\n$\\mu=1/4,1/2,1,2$ (standard errors $0.002$--$0.006$), and 13 of 16 pairs of\nseries matched in $e$ to $\\pm0.05$ differ by more than twice their error;\n$\\beta^{*}$ has the same dependence ($0.180$, $0.169$, $0.147$, $0.123$), and\nover 41 series $b=0.057+0.984\\,\\beta^{*}$ with residual $0.024$. Nor is the\nridge where the first moment is loosest: on 25 series at 50--75 customers\nthe gap between the certified optimum and the floor peaks at $e=2.2$--$5.3$,\non the dense side, with the ridge on a plateau within one stack of the\nmaximum. What peaks on the ridge is the number of closed sets the search can\nreach, not the slack of the bound.\n\n\\paragraph{Twenty-four industrial instances.}\nThe 24 SCOOP instances \\citep{Frinhani2018} have excess $0.97$ to $2.50$,\nmedian $1.35$; 19 lie in $[1,2)$, none has a forest $B$, ten decompose. Every\nrefutation on record for them took at most $22{,}433$ nodes, and the greedy\nclosing rule is exact on 12 and at most $0.061n$ above the optimum. One, with\n60 customers, 49 patterns and optimum 7, sits two stacks below the floor a\nrandom cover with its pattern sizes has at confidence $0.01$: no\ncontradiction, the floor being a random-model statement, but a measure of\ndistance from random, on which the designed Challenge instances sit up to 39\nstacks below. These are 24 instances from one source, read with pieces as\ncustomers (the orientation of the files' format line, the opposite of their\ndescription's prose); we say nothing about industry beyond them.\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.96\\linewidth]{excess_phase_diagram.pdf}\n\\caption{The excess phase diagram. Above, where the instances of\nSection~\\ref{sec:results} sit: the 24 SCOOP instances; the Chu \\& Stuckey\nclasses with $m=n$ as the median of each density over all sizes; the median\nand quartiles of the other collections. Dashed, $e_g(1)=0.62$; solid, $e=1$;\nthe band, the ridge $e=2$--$2.4$. Below, random covers with $m=n$ patterns of\n$d=2$ to $10$ customers: $\\beta^{*}$ (a theorem), the certified slope $b$ at\n30--100 customers with its two-standard-error bar (measured), and $\\beta^{+}$\nof the analysed layouts; in grey, $b$ at $m=n/2$ and $m=2n$, which differ from\nit at equal excess. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{paper1/latex/figures/excess\\_phase\\_diagram.py}.}\n\\label{fig:excess}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\"\"\"\ns = s[:start] + sub + s[end:]\np.write_text(s)\nprint(\"rewritten\", len(sub.split()), \"words\")\nPYEOF\nsed -i 's/figsize=(6.4, 5.6)/figsize=(6.4, 4.9)/; s/\"height_ratios\": \\[1.0, 1.35\\]/\"height_ratios\": [0.9, 1.3]/' paper1/latex/figures/excess_phase_diagram.py && nice -n 19 python3 paper1/latex/figures/excess_phase_diagram.py draw 2>&1 | grep -v Warning | tail -1","description":"Rewrite the subsection shorter and redraw a shorter figure"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1717,"cache_read_input_tokens":303513,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1717,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b64b2670-263a-4912-8a4a-d8fc3e1ebe26","uuid":"66476528-b295-4c8c-b756-8a2ffc54be02","timestamp":"2026-11-01T08:01:28.398Z","request_id":"req_011Cgbrcg7d9FjZk9jobzEga","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_016TPb8UAmPNAQLH5tKtbJ5R":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP && python3 - <<'PYEOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('paper1/latex/sec9e_appendix_random.tex'); s=p.read_text()\nstart = s.index(r\"\\subsection{The excess, and a phase diagram}\")\nend = s.index(r\"\\subsection{What is checked, and what is known}\")\nsub = r\"\"\"\\subsection{The excess, and a phase diagram}\n\\label{app:excess}\n\nThe quantity that ends the previous subsection deserves a name and an axis.\nFor an instance with $n$ customers, $m$ patterns and $N$ ones in the table,\nthe \\emph{excess} is $e=(N-m)/n$, the mean number of patterns per customer\nminus one. With $r=m/n$ and $c=N/m$ the mean customers per pattern,\n$e=r(c-1)$; and if the customer--pattern incidence graph $B$, with a node per\ncustomer and per pattern and an edge per one in the table, has $\\kappa$\ncomponents, it has $n(e-1)+\\kappa$ independent cycles, so $B$ can be a forest\nonly when $e<1$. We read $e$ from the table, never from a generator's nominal\nparameters, whose repairs inflate it. Three values of $e$ carry a meaning.\nThe constant $\\beta^{*}(d,\\mu)$ is positive exactly when $\\mu(d-1)>1$, that\nis when $e>1$ (on paper, from the exponent of~\\leanref{clique-cover}). On\nrandom covers of 15 to 75 customers at every ratio $m/n$