nflate it. Three values","-of $e$ carry a meaning, and Figure~\\ref{fig:excess} places every instance of","-Section~\\ref{sec:results} against them.","-\\begin{itemize}","-\\item $e=1$. The constant $\\beta^{*}(d,\\mu)$ of the previous subsection is","-  positive exactly when $\\mu(d-1)>1$, that is when $e>1$; proved on paper from","-  the exponent of the Lean inequality~\\leanref{clique-cover}.","-\\item $e$ from $2$ to $2.4$. On random covers of 15 to 75 customers, at every","-  ratio $m/n$ from $1/8$ to $2$, the nodes our search spends to refute","-  $\\mathrm{optimum}-1$ peak there, falling by two orders of magnitude on","-  either side (measured).","-\\item $e_g(r)=(\\sqrt{r^{2}+4r}-r)/2$, equal to $0.62$ at $m=n$. There the","-  branching factor of the random incidence graph with patterns of a fixed","-  size is one. On generated covers of 10 to 75 customers the share of","-  instances whose $B$ is a forest falls from $0.74$--$0.92$ below $e_g(r)$ to","-  $0.23$--$0.40$ between $e_g(r)$ and $1$, and is zero above $1$, as the","-  identity forces (measured).","-\\end{itemize}","-Four regimes follow. Below $e_g(r)$ the incidence graph is usually a forest","-and the open stacks are governed by the largest pattern","-(Theorem~\\ref{thm:blowup}). Between $e_g(r)$ and $1$ the giant component is","-there but the first moment says nothing, $\\beta^{*}=0$, and the deterministic","-bounds of Section~\\ref{sec:results} are all there is. Between $1$ and about","-$2.4$ the first moment is positive, the certified optimum per customer sits","-$0.05$--$0.10$ above $\\beta^{*}$, and the cost of certification climbs to the","-ridge; the $d=2$ classes of Section~\\ref{sec:results} sit at $e=1.66$--$1.78$,","-on the sparse side of the ridge, and so do 23 of the 24 industrial instances.","-Above the ridge the cost falls and $\\beta^{*}$ comes within $0.04$--$0.06$ of","-the certified slope at $e\\ge6$ ($m=n$, 30--100 customers); the $d=4$ classes,","-at $e=2.9$--$3.7$, are the ridge's dense shoulder. No benchmark instance we","-hold has $e<e_g(r)$, and only $12$ of $6{,}376$ have $e<1$. The rest of this","-subsection says what is proved and what is measured in each regime.","+the \\emph{excess} is $e=(N-m)/n$, the mean number of patterns per customer","+minus one. With $r=m/n$ and $c=N/m$ the mean customers per pattern,","+$e=r(c-1)$; and if the customer--pattern incidence graph $B$, with a node per","+customer and per pattern and an edge per one in the table, has $\\kappa$","+components, it has $n(e-1)+\\kappa$ independent cycles, so $B$ can be a forest","+only when $e<1$. We read $e$ from the table, never from a generator's nominal","+parameters, whose repairs inflate it. Three values of $e$ carry a meaning.","+The constant $\\beta^{*}(d,\\mu)$ is positive exactly when $\\mu(d-1)>1$, that","+is when $e>1$ (on paper, from the exponent of~\\leanref{clique-cover}). On","+random covers of 15 to 75 customers at every ratio $m/n$ from $1/8$ to $2$,","+the nodes our search spends to refute $\\mathrm{optimum}-1$ peak at $e=2$ to","+$2.4$, falling by two orders of magnitude on either side (measured). And at","+$e_g(r)=(\\sqrt{r^{2}+4r}-r)/2$, $0.62$ for $m=n$, the branching factor of the","+random incidence graph is one: on generated covers of 10 to 75 customers the","+share of instances whose $B$ is a forest falls from $0.74$--$0.92$ below","+$e_g(r)$ to $0.23$--$0.40$ between $e_g(r)$ and $1$, and is zero above","+(measured). Figure~\\ref{fig:excess} places every instance of","+Section~\\ref{sec:results} against the three. Below $e_g(r)$ the open stacks","+are governed by the largest pattern (Theorem~\\ref{thm:blowup}); between","+$e_g(r)$ and $1$ the first moment says nothing and the deterministic bounds","+are all there is; between $1$ and the ridge the certified optimum per","+customer sits $0.05$--$0.10$ above $\\beta^{*}$ and the cost of certification","+climbs, and this is where the $d=2$ classes of Section~\\ref{sec:results}","+($e=1.66$--$1.78$) and 23 of the 24 industrial instances sit; above the ridge","+the cost falls and $\\beta^{*}$ comes within $0.04$--$0.06$ of the certified","+slope at $e\\ge6$ ($m=n$, 30--100 customers), the $d=4$ classes","+($e=2.9$--$3.7$) being the dense shoulder. No benchmark instance we hold has","+$e<e_g(r)$, and 12 of $6{,}376$ have $e<1$."," "," \\paragraph{The forest regime.}"," Write $\\omega$ for the size of the largest pattern."," \\begin{theorem}[blow-up]"," \\label{thm:blowup}"," For every instance, $\\mathrm{MOSP}\\le\\omega\\,(\\pw(B)+1)$. If $B$ is a forest,","-\\[","-  \\mathrm{MOSP}\\;\\le\\;\\omega\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\log_{3}\\tfrac{6(n+m)+3}{5}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr).","-\\]","+$\\mathrm{MOSP}\\le\\omega\\bigl(\\lfloor\\log_{3}\\tfrac{6(n+m)+3}{5}\\rfloor+1\\bigr)$."," \\end{theorem}","-Take a path decomposition of $B$ and replace each pattern node in each bag by","-the pattern's customers. A customer's nodes in $B$ form a star, so the bags","-that now contain the customer are still an interval, and each bag has grown","-by a factor of at most $\\omega$. Nothing about $B$ is used; the forest enters","-only through the pathwidth of a forest on $N$ vertices, which is at most","-$\\lfloor\\log_{3}((6N+3)/5)\\rfloor$, the least $k$ with $N\\le(5\\cdot3^{k}-3)/2$,","-and that bound is sharp. Both statements are Lean","-theorems~\\leanref{blowup}~\\leanref{forest}. The bound is loose. On 7,004","-graphs whose pathwidth we computed exactly, clique trees to 936 vertices,","-random block trees to 120 customers, 3,448 certified random covers with a","-forest incidence graph and 2,000 small random instances, most of them not","-forests, the first inequality is never violated and never exact, and on the","-block-graph families it is 2 to 5.5 times the truth; on the 3,448 random","-forests the pathwidth is $\\omega-1$ on $99.8\\%$ of them, so below $e_g(r)$ the","-optimum is, in practice, the largest pattern (measured). The exact theory is","-known: node search on block graphs is polynomial","-\\citep{ChouKoHoChen2008}. On clique trees, one root clique $K_s$ with $b$","-cliques hung at every customer to depth $h$, the exact values are","-multiplicative in $\\omega$ and the depth, about $(\\omega-2)/2$ per level, and","-no formula linear in $\\omega$ and $h$ fits them; a closed form we fitted to 29","-values failed on the 30th, and we conjecture none. A random corollary, that","-below $e_g(r)$ the optimum is $O(\\omega\\log\\log n)$ with high probability,","-needs the size of the largest component of $B$, which we cite and do not","-prove.","+Replace each pattern node in each bag of a path decomposition of $B$ by the","+pattern's customers: a customer's nodes in $B$ form a star, so its bags stay","+an interval, and no bag grows by more than a factor $\\omega$. Nothing about","+$B$ is used; the forest enters only through the pathwidth of a forest on $N$","+vertices, at most $\\lfloor\\log_{3}((6N+3)/5)\\rfloor$, which is sharp. Both are","+Lean theorems~\\leanref{blowup}~\\leanref{forest}. The bound is loose: on 7,004","+graphs with exact pathwidth (clique trees to 936 vertices, random block trees,","+3,448 certified random covers whose $B$ is a forest, and 2,000 small random","+instances) it is never violated and never exact, 2 to 5.5 times the truth on","+the block-graph families; on the 3,448 random forests the pathwidth is","+$\\omega-1$ on $99.8\\%$, so below $e_g(r)$ the optimum is, in practice, the","+largest pattern (measured). The exact theory on block graphs is polynomial","+\\citep{ChouKoHoChen2008}; on clique trees the exact values grow by about","+$(\\omega-2)/2$ per level of depth, no formula linear in $\\omega$ and the depth","+fits them, and we conjecture no closed form."," "," \\paragraph{Constructions from above.}","-The first moment bounds $\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n$ from below. From above, two","-layouts can be analysed."," \\begin{lemma}[layers]"," \\label{lem:layer}"," If $L_0,L_1,\\dots$ are the distance layers from any vertex of a connected"," graph, $\\pw\\le\\max_i(|L_i|+|L_{i+1}|)-1$; and $\\pw\\le n-\\alpha$ for any"," independent set of $\\alpha$ vertices."," \\end{lemma}","-The bags are $L_i\\cup L_{i+1}$ in the first case and $(V\\setminus I)\\cup\\{v\\}$","-for $v\\in I$ in the second; both are Lean theorems~\\leanref{layer}~\\leanref{indep}.","-On the uniform cover model with $m=\\mu n$ patterns of size $d$, the layer","-sizes follow an exploration recursion with two types of node, and the greedy","-independent set that always takes the customer with fewest patterns follows a","-differential equation; the smaller of their two limits is a constant","+The bags are $L_i\\cup L_{i+1}$, and $(V\\setminus I)\\cup\\{v\\}$ for $v\\in","+I$~\\leanref{layer}~\\leanref{indep}. On the uniform cover model with $m=\\mu n$","+patterns of size $d$ the layer sizes follow a two-type exploration recursion","+and the greedy independent set that takes the customer with fewest patterns","+follows a differential equation; the smaller limit is a constant"," $\\beta^{+}(d,\\mu)$, derived on paper up to the concentration step, which we","-cite and do not re-prove. The layer layout wins below $e=1$, where the giant","-component is small, and the greedy from $e=1.5$ on. Table~\\ref{tab:excess} gives","-$\\beta^{+}$ beside $\\beta^{*}$ and the slope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ fitted","-to the certified instances at 30--100 customers. The sandwich","+cite and do not re-prove. Table~\\ref{tab:excess} gives it beside $\\beta^{*}$","+and the slope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ on the certified instances at","+30--100 customers. The sandwich"," $\\beta^{*}\\le\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n\\le\\limsup\\mathrm{opt}/n\\le\\beta^{+}$ has"," width below $0.12$ at $e\\ge14$ and $0.42$--$0.49$ for $1\\le e\\le3.5$: it says","-least exactly where the search works hardest. The greedy closing rule we use","-for upper bounds, which closes the customer that opens fewest stacks, cannot","-be analysed this way; at 5,000 customers its width per customer sits","-$0.06$--$0.36$ below $\\beta^{+}$ on the cells where its extrapolation agrees","-with the certified slope (measured, 100--5,000 customers, no exact solve).","+least where the search works hardest. The greedy closing rule we use for","+upper bounds cannot be analysed this way; at 5,000 customers its width per","+customer is $0.06$--$0.36$ below $\\beta^{+}$ (measured, no exact solve)."," "," \\begin{table}[htbp]"," \\centering"," \\caption{Random covers with $m=n$ patterns of $d$ customers: the excess, the","-first-moment constant (a lower bound on $\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n$, proved), the","-slope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ fitted to 600--625 certified instances at","-30--100 customers (measured; one standard error in parentheses), and the","-constant of the analysed layouts (an upper bound on $\\limsup\\mathrm{opt}/n$,","-proved on paper up to concentration).}","+first-moment constant (a proved lower bound on $\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n$), the","+slope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ on 600--625 certified instances at 30--100","+customers (measured; one standard error in parentheses), and the constant of","+the analysed layouts (an upper bound on $\\limsup\\mathrm{opt}/n$, on paper up","+to concentration).}"," \\label{tab:excess}"," \\small"," \\input{tables/excess_beta}"," \\end{table}"," ","-\\paragraph{Why the first moment is loose, and what does not fix it.}","-A layout of width $k$ forces not one empty pair but $\\ell$ nested ones, one at","-each of $\\ell$ prefix sizes. Counting that structure with the exact","-inclusion--exclusion factor per pattern gives a constant $\\beta^{*}_{\\ell}$","-that is \\emph{lower} than $\\beta^{*}$ on every cell we computed, by $0.051$","-at $\\ell=2$ for $d=6$, $\\mu=1$, and lower still for $\\ell=3,4$, at every","-spacing of the pairs: naming an extra cell of $\\varepsilon n$ vertices costs","-$\\Theta(\\varepsilon\\log(1/\\varepsilon))$ of entropy per customer and buys","+\\paragraph{Why the first moment is loose.}","+A layout of width $k$ forces $\\ell$ nested empty pairs, one per prefix size,","+but counting them gives a constant \\emph{below} $\\beta^{*}$ on every cell (by","+$0.051$ at $\\ell=2$, $d=6$, $\\mu=1$, and more for larger $\\ell$, at every","+spacing): naming $\\varepsilon n$ more vertices costs","+$\\Theta(\\varepsilon\\log(1/\\varepsilon))$ of entropy and buys"," $\\Theta(\\varepsilon)$ of constraint. The slack is in the union bound. The","-empty pairs cluster, $E[X^{2}]/E[X]^{2}=e^{0.23n}$ at $\\beta^{*}$ for $d=6$,","-$\\mu=1$, and on 650 certified instances at 30--75 customers the empty-pair","-condition itself is within a stack of the pathwidth while the floor sits one","-to three stacks lower. A second moment bounds $\\Pr[X>0]$ from the wrong side;","-a count over one representative per cluster of pairs is the route we have not","-taken.","+empty pairs cluster ($E[X^{2}]/E[X]^{2}=e^{0.23n}$ at $\\beta^{*}$ for $d=6$,","+$\\mu=1$), and on 650 certified instances at 30--75 customers the empty-pair","+condition is within a stack of the pathwidth while the floor sits one to","+three stacks lower. A second moment bounds $\\Pr[X>0]$ from the wrong side; a","+count over one representative per cluster is the route not taken."," "," \\paragraph{One parameter, or two.}"," The excess sets the threshold, the ridge and the scale, not the value. At","-equal excess the certified slope falls as the patterns get smaller and more","-numerous: at $e=2$ it is $0.282$, $0.232$, $0.200$ and $0.167$ for","-$\\mu=1/4,1/2,1,2$, with standard errors $0.002$--$0.006$, and 13 of the 16","-pairs of series matched in $e$ to $\\pm0.05$ differ by more than twice their","-error. The first moment has the same dependence, $0.180$, $0.169$, $0.147$,","-$0.123$ at those four cells, and over 41 series $b=0.057+0.984\\,\\beta^{*}$","-with a residual of $0.024$: $\\beta^{*}(d,\\mu)$ is the better one-parameter","-coordinate, and $e$ is the coordinate of the lines. The ridge is not where","-the first moment is loosest. On 25 series at 50--75 customers the gap between","-the certified optimum and the floor peaks at $e=2.2$--$5.3$, on the dense","-side, with the ridge sitting on a plateau within one stack of that maximum on","-every series. What peaks on the ridge is the number of closed sets the search","-can reach by feasible steps, not the slack of the bound.","+$e=2$ the certified slope is $0.282$, $0.232$, $0.200$, $0.167$ for","+$\\mu=1/4,1/2,1,2$ (standard errors $0.002$--$0.006$), and 13 of 16 pairs of","+series matched in $e$ to $\\pm0.05$ differ by more than twice their error;","+$\\beta^{*}$ has the same dependence ($0.180$, $0.169$, $0.147$, $0.123$), and","+over 41 series $b=0.057+0.984\\,\\beta^{*}$ with residual $0.024$. Nor is the","+ridge where the first moment is loosest: on 25 series at 50--75 customers","+the gap between the certified optimum and the floor peaks at $e=2.2$--$5.3$,","+on the dense side, with the ridge on a plateau within one stack of the","+maximum. What peaks on the ridge is the number of closed sets the search can","+reach, not the slack of the bound."," "," \\paragraph{Twenty-four industrial instances.}","-The 24 SCOOP instances \\citep{Frinhani2018}, from two sawmilling companies,","-have excess $0.97$ to $2.50$, median $1.35$; 19 lie in $[1,2)$, three on the","-ridge, one above, one between $e_g(r)$ and $1$; none has a forest incidence","-graph and ten decompose into independent components. Every refutation of","-$\\mathrm{optimum}-1$ on record for them took at most $22{,}433$ nodes, and the","-greedy closing rule is exact on 12 of the 24 and at most $0.061n$ above the","-optimum. One instance, with 60 customers, 49 patterns and optimum 7, sits two","-stacks below the first-moment floor that a random cover with its pattern","-sizes would have at confidence $0.01$: a random cover of that size law has","-optimum at most 7 with probability below $7\\times10^{-5}$. That is not a","-contradiction, the floor being a statement about the random model; it is a","-measurement of distance from random, and the designed instances of the 2005","-Challenge sit up to 39 stacks below theirs. These are 24 instances from one","-source, read with pieces as customers and patterns as patterns, the","-orientation of the files' format line and the opposite of their description's","-prose; we say nothing about industry beyond them.","+The 24 SCOOP instances \\citep{Frinhani2018} have excess $0.97$ to $2.50$,","+median $1.35$; 19 lie in $[1,2)$, none has a forest $B$, ten decompose. Every","+refutation on record for them took at most $22{,}433$ nodes, and the greedy","+closing rule is exact on 12 and at most $0.061n$ above the optimum. One, with","+60 customers, 49 patterns and optimum 7, sits two stacks below the floor a","+random cover with its pattern sizes has at confidence $0.01$: no","+contradiction, the floor being a random-model statement, but a measure of","+distance from random, on which the designed Challenge instances sit up to 39","+stacks below. These are 24 instances from one source, read with pieces as","+customers (the orientation of the files' format line, the opposite of their","+description's prose); we say nothing about industry beyond them."," "," \\begin{figure}[htbp]"," \\centering","-\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{excess_phase_diagram.pdf}","+\\includegraphics[width=0.96\\linewidth]{excess_phase_diagram.pdf}"," \\caption{The excess phase diagram. Above, where the instances of"," Section~\\ref{sec:results} sit: the 24 SCOOP instances; the Chu \\& Stuckey"," classes with $m=n$ as the median of each density over all sizes; the median","-and quartiles of the other collections. The dashed line is $e_g(1)=0.62$, the","-solid line $e=1$, the band $e=2$--$2.4$ the ridge. Below, random covers with","-$m=n$ patterns of $d$ customers, $d=2$ to $10$: the first-moment constant","-$\\beta^{*}$ (a theorem), the slope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ on certified","-instances at 30--100 customers with its two-standard-error bar (measured), and","-the constant $\\beta^{+}$ of the analysed layouts; in grey, $b$ at $m=n/2$ and","-$m=2n$, which differ from it at equal excess. Regenerate:","+and quartiles of the other collections. Dashed, $e_g(1)=0.62$; solid, $e=1$;","+the band, the ridge $e=2$--$2.4$. Below, random covers with $m=n$ patterns of","+$d=2$ to $10$ customers: $\\beta^{*}$ (a theorem), the certified slope $b$ at","+30--100 customers with its two-standard-error bar (measured), and $\\beta^{+}$","+of the analysed layouts; in grey, $b$ at $m=n/2$ and $m=2n$, which differ from","+it at equal excess. Regenerate:"," \\texttt{paper1/latex/figures/excess\\_phase\\_diagram.py}.}"," \\label{fig:excess}"," \\end{figure}"]}]},{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper1/latex/figures/excess_phase_diagram.py","hunks":[{"oldStart":143,"oldLines":8,"newStart":143,"newLines":8,"lines":["     from matplotlib.lines import Line2D"," ","     plt.rcParams.update({\"font.family\": \"serif\", \"font.size\": 8, \"pdf.fonttype\": 42})","-    fig, (top, bot) = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(6.4, 5.6), sharex=True,","-                                   gridspec_kw={\"height_ratios\": [1.0, 1.35], \"hspace\": 0.12})","+    fig, (top, bot) = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(6.4, 4.9), sharex=True,","+                                   gridspec_kw={\"height_ratios\": [0.9, 1.3], \"hspace\": 0.12})"," ","     # --- top: where the instances sit -------------------------------------------------","     rows = [(\"SCOOP (24)\", \"scoop\", None),"]}]}],"moreFiles":2,"changedFiles":["/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper1/latex/sec9e_appendix_random.tex","/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper1/latex/figures/excess_phase_diagram.py","/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper1/figures/excess_phase_diagram.pdf","/home/al/dev/MOSP/reports/figures/excess_phase_diagram.png"]}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016TPb8UAmPNAQLH5tKtbJ5R","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"